== SPORT: the DSP--FPGA serial data link

This chapter is the single source for every FPGA bitstream the SPORT
missions use. The link carries a PRBS-31 stream between the ADSP-21569
DSP and the iCE40-HX8K FPGA over the DSP's synchronous serial ports
(SPORTs), and verifies it bit-for-bit at the receiving end. One FPGA
top module, `sport_bidir`, covers all eight tested directions --- 1-,
2-, and 4-lane DSP$\rightarrow$FPGA (D2F), the symmetric FPGA$\rightarrow$DSP
(F2D), and the 1x1 and 2x2 bidirectional cases --- selected entirely by
parameters. It is the FPGA counterpart of the single DSP application in
`adsp2156/sport`.

Every lane is a one-bit-per-clock PRBS-31 stream framed into 32-bit
words. There is no handshake, training sequence, or descrambler: both
endpoints run the same LFSR from the same seed, stepped by the same
bit clock, so they stay in lockstep and the receiver simply compares
the wire against its own locally generated word. The DSP is always the
bus master and owns the bit clock; the FPGA never generates a clock of
its own (no PLL in the data path).

`sport_bidir` is built from three pieces, each developed in its own
section below:

  - `sport_tx_sync_nopll` --- the *no-PLL source-synchronous
    transmitter* (`to_dsp`). It generates a PRBS-31 lane clocked by the
    DSP's forwarded clock, so it is in lockstep with the DSP's receiver
    with no clock of its own. This is what every mission uses for an F2D
    lane.

  - `sport_tx_prbs_ser` --- a *free-running* transmitter that makes its
    own bit clock with a PLL. It predates the no-PLL transmitter and the
    missions no longer select it, but `sport_bidir` keeps it as the
    alternate clocking strategy, so it is documented here too.

  - `sport_rx` --- the *receiver* (`from_dsp`). It samples a DSP-driven
    lane on the forwarded clock, reassembles 32-bit words, checks each
    against its own PRBS-31 LFSR, and reports the running word and error
    counts over UART. This is what verifies a D2F lane.

=== The PRBS-31 stream

The payload is the maximum-length 31-bit linear-feedback sequence of
the primitive polynomial $x^{31} + x^{28} + 1$. A 31-bit shift register
`lfsr` holds the state; the next bit is the XOR of the two tap bits
`lfsr[30] ^ lfsr[27]`, shifted in at the low end. Seeded to all-ones
(`31'h7FFFFFFF` --- any non-zero seed works, an LFSR cannot leave the
zero state) it cycles through all $2^{31}-1$ non-zero states before
repeating. One state advances per bit, so at the 59.375 Mbps lane rate
the sequence repeats only every $(2^{31}-1)/59.375\text{e}6 \approx 36$
seconds --- far longer than any single mission word, so no lane ever
sees the pattern wrap.

Two conventions are fixed across the whole link and must match on both
ends:

  - *MSB-first.* Each 32-bit word is the next 32 successive LFSR output
    bits, sent most-significant first. A transmitter that emits one
    `lfsr[30]^lfsr[27]` bit per clock and a receiver that shifts each
    received bit into the bottom of a 32-bit register therefore agree on
    word boundaries without any alignment search.

  - *Word zero is the first frame.* The transmitter holds its LFSR at
    the seed until the link goes live, so the very first 32-bit word the
    receiver sees is the seed word. The frame-sync line `afs` marks bit0
    of every word for the DSP's late-frame-sync SPORT framing.

Because the transmitter sends 32 successive LFSR bits per word and
advances the LFSR 32 steps per word, a serial one-bit-per-clock LFSR
and a 32-bit-at-a-time word generator emit byte-identical waveforms.
Both styles appear below (the serial form in the no-PLL transmitter,
the word form historically in `sport_tx_prbs_ser`), and the receiver
checks either against the same locally stepped LFSR.

=== The no-PLL source-synchronous transmitter

`sport_tx_sync_nopll` drives one F2D lane. The DSP is the clock master:
it forwards its SPORT bit clock to the FPGA as `fwd_aclk`, and this
module clocks everything from that --- there is no PLL and no
free-running clock anywhere in the transmit path. The DSP's receiver
LFSR and this LFSR both start at the seed and are stepped by the same
clock edges, so they advance bit-for-bit together with nothing to lose
lock to.

The module holds `lfsr` at the seed while `run` is inactive. When `run`
asserts, a six-bit `warm` counter waits 63 stable forwarded-clock ticks
before releasing the LFSR, so that word zero lands on the first frame
after the clock is known good rather than on a startup glitch. The
`run` input, which crosses from the DSP-controlled domain, is taken
through a two-stage synchronizer `run_s`.

<<sport_tx_sync_nopll.v>>=
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
// Copyright (c) 2026 Jakob Kastelic
module sport_tx_sync_nopll #(
      parameter DATA_NEG = 1,
      parameter CLK_POS  = 1
   )(
      input  fwd_aclk,
      input  run,
      output ad0_out,
      output aclk_out,
      output afs_out
   );
   reg [1:0] run_s = 2'b00;
   reg [5:0] warm  = 6'd0;
   localparam [30:0] PRBS31_SEED = 31'h7FFFFFFF;

   reg [30:0] lfsr   = PRBS31_SEED;
   reg [4:0]  bitcnt = 5'd0;
   reg        ad0_p  = 1'b0;
   reg        afs_p  = 1'b0;

   <<Step the PRBS LFSR one bit per forwarded clock>>
   <<Launch the lane on source-synchronous output pads>>
endmodule
@

While warming or idle the LFSR reloads the seed and the outputs sit
low. Once warm, one PRBS bit is emitted per forwarded-clock period:
`ad0_p` takes the next tap XOR, `afs_p` pulses on the bit-0 of each
word (`bitcnt` wraps 31$\rightarrow$0 on its own), and the LFSR shifts
in the bit it just sent.

<<Step the PRBS LFSR one bit per forwarded clock>>=
always @(posedge fwd_aclk) begin
   run_s <= {run_s[0], run};
   if (!run_s[1] || warm != 6'd63) begin
      if (run_s[1])
         warm <= warm + 6'd1;
      else
         warm <= 6'd0;
      lfsr   <= PRBS31_SEED;
      bitcnt <= 5'd0;
      ad0_p  <= 1'b0;
      afs_p  <= 1'b0;
   end else begin
      ad0_p  <= lfsr[30] ^ lfsr[27];
      afs_p  <= (bitcnt == 5'd0);
      lfsr   <= {lfsr[29:0], (lfsr[30] ^ lfsr[27])};
      bitcnt <= bitcnt + 5'd1;
   end
end
@

The three output pads are the only part of the link whose analog
timing was tuned. Data and frame-sync are launched through matched
`SB_IO` output flops on the falling edge of `fwd_aclk` (`DATA_NEG`),
half a bit period away from the DSP receiver's rising-edge sample so
the sample lands in the centre of the eye and away from the
simultaneous-switching-output disturbance. The forwarded clock
`aclk_out` is a DDR output that rises at the posedge (`CLK_POS`), so it
arrives centred on the data. The two phase knobs `DATA_NEG` and
`CLK_POS` are the parameters that were swept to find that alignment.

<<Launch the lane on source-synchronous output pads>>=
SB_IO #(.PIN_TYPE(6'b010100), .NEG_TRIGGER(DATA_NEG ? 1'b1 : 1'b0)) ad0_io (
   .PACKAGE_PIN(ad0_out), .OUTPUT_CLK(fwd_aclk), .D_OUT_0(ad0_p)
);
SB_IO #(.PIN_TYPE(6'b010100), .NEG_TRIGGER(DATA_NEG ? 1'b1 : 1'b0)) afs_io (
   .PACKAGE_PIN(afs_out), .OUTPUT_CLK(fwd_aclk), .D_OUT_0(afs_p)
);
SB_IO #(.PIN_TYPE(6'b010000)) aclk_io (
   .PACKAGE_PIN(aclk_out), .OUTPUT_CLK(fwd_aclk),
   .D_OUT_0(CLK_POS ? 1'b1 : 1'b0), .D_OUT_1(CLK_POS ? 1'b0 : 1'b1)
);
@

=== The free-running transmitter

`sport_tx_prbs_ser` is the older F2D transmitter and the alternative
`sport_bidir` instantiates when `SYNC_TX` is zero. Where the no-PLL
transmitter borrows the DSP's clock, this one makes its own bit clock
with an `SB_PLL40_CORE` from the 12 MHz reference and free-runs. The
missions all select the no-PLL transmitter now, so this module is no
longer on the critical path, but it is kept as the second clocking
strategy and so is documented here.

The PRBS payload is generated by a one-bit-per-clock serial LFSR ---
the same recurrence and seed as everywhere else, so the waveform is
identical to a word-at-a-time generator and the receiver cannot tell
the two apart. (The serial form replaced an earlier word-at-a-time
generator with two deep combinational chains; dropping those freed
enough LUTs and routing for a co-located `from_dsp` receiver to place
cleanly in the bidirectional builds.)

A single channel, `prbs_chan_ser`, runs a three-phase startup before
the live stream. It first emits 8192 dummy words of a fixed pattern
(`0xA5A55A5A`), then holds the line idle for 200000 clocks, then begins
the PRBS pattern. The dummy/idle preamble gives the DSP-side framing a
known run-up before word zero. Each phase counts 32-bit words via
`bitcnt`, and `afs_r` pulses on bit0 of every word exactly as on the
live stream. The channel launches on the *negedge* of `pll_clk`, half a
bit period from the DSP receiver's posedge sample --- the same
mid-bit-eye launch the no-PLL transmitter gets from its `SB_IO` negedge
flops, but here built into the logic clock edge since this stream rides
its own PLL clock rather than a forwarded one.

<<Free-running PRBS channel>>=
module prbs_chan_ser (
      input  pll_clk,
      input  enable,
      output reg ad0_r,
      output reg afs_r
   );
   localparam [30:0] PRBS31_SEED = 31'h7fffffff;
   localparam [31:0] DUMMY_WORD  = 32'hA5A55A5A;
   localparam [13:0] DUMMY_WORDS = 14'd8192;
   localparam [21:0] IDLE_CYCLES = 22'd200000;
   localparam [1:0]  PH_DUMMY    = 2'd0;
   localparam [1:0]  PH_IDLE     = 2'd1;
   localparam [1:0]  PH_PATTERN  = 2'd2;

   reg [4:0]  bitcnt      = 5'd0;
   reg [1:0]  phase       = PH_DUMMY;
   reg [13:0] dummy_count = 14'd0;
   reg [21:0] idle_count  = 22'd0;
   reg [30:0] prbs_state  = PRBS31_SEED;
   reg [31:0] shift_word  = 32'd0;
   reg        nb;

   always @(negedge pll_clk) begin
      if (!enable) begin
         ad0_r <= 1'b0;
         afs_r <= 1'b0;
         bitcnt <= 5'd0;
         phase <= PH_DUMMY;
         dummy_count <= 14'd0;
         idle_count <= 22'd0;
         prbs_state <= PRBS31_SEED;
         shift_word <= 32'd0;
      end else if (phase == PH_IDLE) begin
         afs_r <= 1'b0; ad0_r <= 1'b0;
         if (idle_count == IDLE_CYCLES - 22'd1) begin
            phase <= PH_PATTERN; idle_count <= 22'd0; bitcnt <= 5'd0;
         end else idle_count <= idle_count + 22'd1;
      end else if (phase == PH_DUMMY) begin
         afs_r <= (bitcnt == 5'd0);
         if (bitcnt == 5'd0) begin
            ad0_r <= DUMMY_WORD[31]; shift_word <= {DUMMY_WORD[30:0], 1'b0};
            bitcnt <= 5'd1;
         end else begin
            ad0_r <= shift_word[31]; shift_word <= {shift_word[30:0], 1'b0};
            if (bitcnt == 5'd31) begin
               bitcnt <= 5'd0;
               if (dummy_count == DUMMY_WORDS - 14'd1) begin
                  phase <= PH_IDLE; dummy_count <= 14'd0;
               end else dummy_count <= dummy_count + 14'd1;
            end else bitcnt <= bitcnt + 5'd1;
         end
      end else begin
         afs_r <= (bitcnt == 5'd0);
         nb = prbs_state[30] ^ prbs_state[27];
         ad0_r <= nb;
         prbs_state <= {prbs_state[29:0], nb};
         bitcnt <= (bitcnt == 5'd31) ? 5'd0 : bitcnt + 5'd1;
      end
   end
endmodule
@

The top wraps that channel: the PLL multiplies the 12 MHz reference up
to the bit clock, an optional `START_DELAY_CYCLES` gate holds the
stream off until the link is up, the channel drives all `N` output
lanes identically, and `TX_QUIET` is a diagnostic that keeps the PLL
and logic placed but parks every pin low.

<<Free-running transmitter top>>=
module sport_tx_prbs_ser #(
      parameter N = 1,
      parameter START_DELAY_CYCLES = 0,
      parameter TX_QUIET = 0
   )(
      input              clk12,
      output [N-1:0]     ad0_out,
      output [N-1:0]     aclk_out,
      output [N-1:0]     afs_out
   );
   wire pll_clk, pll_lock;
   SB_PLL40_CORE #(
      .FEEDBACK_PATH("SIMPLE"),
      .DIVR(4'd0),
      .DIVF(7'd82),
      .DIVQ(3'd4),
      .FILTER_RANGE(3'd1)
   ) pll (
      .REFERENCECLK(clk12), .PLLOUTCORE(pll_clk), .LOCK(pll_lock),
      .RESETB(1'b1), .BYPASS(1'b0)
   );

   wire stream_enable;
   generate
      if (START_DELAY_CYCLES == 0) begin : no_delay
         assign stream_enable = 1'b1;
      end else begin : delayed_start
         reg [31:0] start_delay = 32'd0;
         reg stream_enable_r = 1'b0;
         always @(posedge clk12) begin
            if (!stream_enable_r) begin
               if (start_delay == START_DELAY_CYCLES - 1)
                  stream_enable_r <= 1'b1;
               else
                  start_delay <= start_delay + 32'd1;
            end
         end
         assign stream_enable = stream_enable_r;
      end
   endgenerate

   wire ad0_w, afs_w;
   wire chan_en = (TX_QUIET != 0) ? 1'b0 : stream_enable;
   prbs_chan_ser g (.pll_clk(pll_clk), .enable(chan_en), .ad0_r(ad0_w), .afs_r(afs_w));

   assign ad0_out = (TX_QUIET != 0) ? {N{1'b0}} : {N{ad0_w}};
   assign afs_out = (TX_QUIET != 0) ? {N{1'b0}} : {N{afs_w}};

   genvar i;
   generate
      for (i = 0; i < N; i = i + 1) begin : ch
         assign aclk_out[i] = (TX_QUIET != 0) ? 1'b0 : pll_clk;
      end
   endgenerate
endmodule
@

Both modules tangle into one file, the channel first so it is defined
before its instantiation:

<<sport_tx_prbs_ser.v>>=
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
// Copyright (c) 2026 Jakob Kastelic
<<Free-running PRBS channel>>
<<Free-running transmitter top>>
@

=== The receiver: sport_rx

`sport_rx` is the D2F verifier. Each of its `N` lanes carries a DSP-driven
PRBS-31 stream framed into 32-bit words on a forwarded bit clock; the module
reassembles those words, checks each against its own locally stepped LFSR,
and once enough words have been seen prints a single PASS/FAIL line over
UART. The two test boards wire lane 0 to SPORT4A on DAI1 (PB01/PB02/PB04 ->
P13/M11/P10) and lane 1 to SPORT0A routed onto DAI0 (PB05/PB07/PB08 ->
R5/T5/R9). After every forwarded clock has been idle for about 5 ms the
report goes out as

----
sport_rx lanes=... per_ch_words_hex=... errors_hex=... PASS
----

on the UART pad (115200 baud, `tx` = B12).

The file is one per-lane checker `sport_rx_chan` followed by the
`N`-lane wrapper `sport_rx` that instantiates it, aggregates the lanes,
and owns the UART. The channel must be defined before the wrapper that
instantiates it, so it tangles first.

<<sport_rx.v>>=
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
// Copyright (c) 2026 Jakob Kastelic
<<The sample-edge selector>>
<<sport_rx_chan: the per-lane checker>>
<<sport_rx: the N-lane wrapper>>
@

The forwarded clock is the only clock the receive logic runs on, and the
edge on which the lane is sampled is a build-time choice. By default the
data and frame-sync are captured on the falling edge of the forwarded
clock --- half a bit away from the launching edge, in the centre of the
eye --- but defining `SAMPLE_POS` moves capture to the rising edge.

<<The sample-edge selector>>=
`ifndef SAMPLE_POS
 `define ACLK_SAMPLE_EDGE negedge
`else
 `define ACLK_SAMPLE_EDGE posedge
`endif
@

==== The per-lane checker

`sport_rx_chan` checks one lane. Its ports split into the inputs from
the lane (`aclk_in`, `ad0_in`, `afs_in`), the `clk12`-domain `run` and
`done` handshake, and a wide bundle of count/diagnostic outputs. `RESYNC`
selects the re-acquiring frame state machine used in the bidirectional
builds; `MIN_DONE_WORDS` is how many good words constitute a completed
transfer; `MSB_FIRST` matches the link's bit order.

<<sport_rx_chan: the per-lane checker>>=
module sport_rx_chan #(
      parameter MIN_DONE_WORDS = 32'h00b00000,
      parameter MSB_FIRST = 1,
      parameter RESYNC = 0
   )(
      input        clk12,
      input        run,
      input        aclk_in,
      input        ad0_in,
      input        afs_in,
      output [31:0] words,
      output [31:0] errors,
      output [31:0] first_rx,
      output [31:0] first_exp,
      output [31:0] first_idx,
      output [31:0] diag0,
      output [31:0] diag1,
      output [31:0] diag2,
      output [31:0] diag3,
      output [31:0] diag4,
      output [31:0] diag5,
      output [31:0] diag6,
      output [31:0] diag7,
      output [31:0] diag8,
      output [31:0] diag9,
      output       done,
      output [31:0] prog_w,
      output [31:0] prog_e,
      output        prog_t,
      output [31:0] fe_idx_o,
      output [31:0] fe_got_o,
      output [31:0] fe_exp_o,
      output        fe_t
   );

   <<sport_rx_chan: progress and first-error snapshot registers>>
   <<sport_rx_chan: sample the lane in the aclk domain>>
   <<sport_rx_chan: PRBS lockstep state>>
   <<sport_rx_chan: next-word and next-expected-word combinational logic>>
   <<sport_rx_chan: the frame state machine and word check>>
   <<sport_rx_chan: cross to clk12 and detect idle>>
   <<sport_rx_chan: drive the outputs>>
endmodule
@

Two snapshots cross to the `clk12` UART domain. The `prog_*` set is a
roughly 1 Hz progress heartbeat: every $2^{21}$ words (8 MiB at 60+ Mbps,
the `PROG_MASK` boundary) the current word and error counts are latched and
`prog_t` toggled. The `fe_*` set is a one-shot first-error capture ---
the word index, the received word, and the expected word at the first
mismatch, with `fe_t` toggled once. Both are level snapshots with a toggle
handshake so the slow domain can latch them without a true CDC FIFO.

<<sport_rx_chan: progress and first-error snapshot registers>>=
   localparam [31:0] PROG_MASK = 32'h001fffff;

   reg [31:0] prog_w_r = 32'd0;
   reg [31:0] prog_e_r = 32'd0;
   reg        prog_t_r = 1'b0;
   reg [31:0] fe_idx_r = 32'd0;
   reg [31:0] fe_got_r = 32'd0;
   reg [31:0] fe_exp_r = 32'd0;
   reg        fe_t_r   = 1'b0;
   reg        fe_seen  = 1'b0;
   assign prog_w = prog_w_r;
   assign prog_e = prog_e_r;
   assign prog_t = prog_t_r;
   assign fe_idx_o = fe_idx_r;
   assign fe_got_o = fe_got_r;
   assign fe_exp_o = fe_exp_r;
   assign fe_t = fe_t_r;
@

The lane's data and frame-sync are sampled into `ad0_r`/`afs_r` on the
selected edge of the forwarded clock. (The wrapper has already registered
the pins in the IO cells; this is the fabric-side copy the checker works
from.)

<<sport_rx_chan: sample the lane in the aclk domain>>=
   wire aclk_global = aclk_in;

   reg ad0_r = 1'b0;
   reg afs_r = 1'b0;
   always @(`ACLK_SAMPLE_EDGE aclk_global) begin
      ad0_r <= ad0_in;
      afs_r <= afs_in;
   end
@

The checker is a pure PRBS-31 lockstep comparator with no sync mechanism.
The DSP transmits 32-bit PRBS-31 words; `run` holds both endpoint LFSRs at
the shared seed `31'h7FFFFFFF`. The first observed frame-sync can include
pad/SRU startup state from before the SPORT stream is fully up, so that one
fixed startup word is discarded without advancing the LFSR. From the next
word on, the checker steps exactly once per 32 DSP bit clocks --- there is
no phase search, training pattern, skipped-word alignment, or data-dependent
re-sync. `lfsr` is the live expected-bit generator; `lfsr_word` snapshots it
at each word boundary so a mid-word abort can rewind to a clean boundary.
`bitpos` counts bits within a word, `started`/`armed`/`arm_wait` run the
acquire sequence, `pass_ready` marks the startup frame as dropped, and
`fs_seen` tracks the first (artifact) frame-sync rise.

<<sport_rx_chan: PRBS lockstep state>>=
   localparam [30:0] PRBS31_SEED = 31'h7FFFFFFF;
   reg [30:0] lfsr   = PRBS31_SEED;
   reg        afs_d  = 1'b0;
   reg [4:0]  bitpos = 5'd0;
   reg        started = 1'b0;
   reg        armed = 1'b0;
   reg [2:0]  arm_wait = 3'd0;
   reg        pass_ready = 1'b0;
   reg        fs_seen = 1'b0;
   reg [30:0] lfsr_word = 31'h7FFFFFFF;
   reg [31:0] shift_word = 32'd0;
   reg [31:0] exp_shift_word = 32'd0;
   reg [31:0] wcount = 32'd0;
   reg [31:0] ecount = 32'd0;
   reg        done_aclk = 1'b0;
   reg        done_toggle = 1'b0;
   reg [31:0] report_words_aclk = 32'd0;
   reg [31:0] report_errors_aclk = 32'd0;
   <<sport_rx_chan: optional diagnostic state>>
@

Several optional diagnostic taps, all off by default, are compiled in by
`define`s. `DIAG_FIRST` records the very first received/expected word and
its index; `DIAG_WORDS` captures the first ten words (or, with
`DIAG_WINDOW_START`, the first ten past a chosen index) into the ten `diag`
outputs; `DIAG_FSPHASE` measures the frame-sync gap; and `DIAG_GAPAUDIT`
verifies every frame spans exactly the nominal number of forwarded-clock
edges, recording any deviation --- an extra or missing edge on the wire ---
into the diag slots.

==== Diagnostic build options

These were all bring-up instruments. *None is set in any shipping
build*, so every block below is `ifdef`-fenced out of the mission
bitstreams and contributes nothing to the synthesized netlist; they are
documented here so a future debugger knows what each one captures and
which ones conflict.

[cols="<,<,<",options="header"]
|===
| macro | what it captures | exclusions / notes
| `DIAG_FIRST`        | first received/expected word + its index | with `DIAG_MISMATCH`, captures only the first *mismatching* word
| `DIAG_MISMATCH`     | narrows `DIAG_FIRST` to the first error | needs `DIAG_FIRST`
| `DIAG_INDEX`        | reports the first-word *index* instead of expected word | needs `DIAG_FIRST`
| `DIAG_WORDS`        | first ten checked words into the ten diag slots | disables the live progress line; mutually exclusive with `DIAG_FSPHASE` and `DIAG_GAPAUDIT` (they own the diag slots)
| `DIAG_WINDOW_START` | start `DIAG_WORDS` capture past a chosen word index | needs `DIAG_WORDS`
| `DIAG_FSPHASE`      | frame-sync gap + bit position at each FS rise; arms `done` after ten captures | owns the diag slots; overrides `DIAG_WORDS`
| `DIAG_GAPAUDIT`     | per-frame edge-count audit (extra/missing wire edges) | owns the diag slots; overrides `DIAG_WORDS`; clobbers `diag_word8/9` with live word/error counts
| `DIAG_STATE`        | mirrors acquire flags into `fe_idx`; repurposes the progress line as a periodic state dump | suppresses the normal first-error line
| `DIAG_RAW`          | reports a raw forwarded-clock edge count as the word count | alternate report trigger; takes priority over `DIAG_FORCE_REPORT`
| `DIAG_FORCE_REPORT` | forces a report after about 10 s instead of on idle | alternate report trigger
| `DIAG_FORCE_LIVE`   | keeps the report registers live (not zeroed on reset) | for reading counts mid-run
|===

<<sport_rx_chan: optional diagnostic state>>=
`ifdef DIAG_FIRST
   reg [31:0] first_rx_r = 32'd0;
   reg [31:0] first_exp_r = 32'd0;
   reg [31:0] first_idx_r = 32'd0;
   reg        first_seen = 1'b0;
`endif
`ifdef DIAG_WORDS
   reg [31:0] diag_word0 = 32'd0;
   reg [31:0] diag_word1 = 32'd0;
   reg [31:0] diag_word2 = 32'd0;
   reg [31:0] diag_word3 = 32'd0;
   reg [31:0] diag_word4 = 32'd0;
   reg [31:0] diag_word5 = 32'd0;
   reg [31:0] diag_word6 = 32'd0;
   reg [31:0] diag_word7 = 32'd0;
   reg [31:0] diag_word8 = 32'd0;
   reg [31:0] diag_word9 = 32'd0;
   reg [3:0]  diag_count = 4'd0;
`ifdef DIAG_FSPHASE
   reg [15:0] fs_gap = 16'd0;
`endif
`ifdef DIAG_GAPAUDIT
   reg [15:0] gap_audit = 16'd0;
   reg [15:0] gap_nominal = 16'd0;
   reg        gap_lock = 1'b0;
   reg [4:0]  fsbit_nominal = 5'd0;
   reg        fsbit_lock = 1'b0;
`endif
 `ifdef DIAG_WINDOW_START
   localparam [31:0] DIAG_BASE = `DIAG_WINDOW_START;
 `else
   localparam [31:0] DIAG_BASE = 32'd0;
 `endif
`endif
@

The expected word and the received word are both assembled
combinationally. `next_word` shifts the just-sampled bit into the running
receive word, MSB-first by default (LSB-first builds set the bit at
`bitpos`). `exp_bit` is the next PRBS output `lfsr[30]^lfsr[27]`, `lfsr_next`
the LFSR after one step, and `next_exp_word` the expected word with that bit
appended. `next_word_bad` is the per-word comparison.

<<sport_rx_chan: next-word and next-expected-word combinational logic>>=
   wire [31:0] next_msb_word = {shift_word[30:0], ad0_r};
   wire [31:0] next_lsb_word = shift_word | (32'h1 << bitpos);
   wire [31:0] next_word = MSB_FIRST ? next_msb_word :
                            (ad0_r ? next_lsb_word : shift_word);

   wire        exp_bit = lfsr[30] ^ lfsr[27];
   wire [30:0] lfsr_next = {lfsr[29:0], exp_bit};
   wire [31:0] next_exp_word = {exp_shift_word[30:0], exp_bit};

   wire        next_word_bad = (next_word != next_exp_word);
@

The heart of the checker is one `always` block on the sample edge. It
delays the frame-sync (`afs_d`) for edge detection, runs the optional
diagnostics, then steps through the acquire/check state machine. The states,
in priority order, are: idle reset while `run` is low and nothing started;
hold the finished report while `run` is low after `done`; the `RESYNC`
dep-FS arm-at-any-rise; the plain arm/`arm_wait`/start sequence; the
`RESYNC` mid-word abort (frame-sync dipped before bit 31, so rewind to the
boundary and re-arm); and the running check itself, which shifts in each
bit, compares whole words at `bitpos == 31`, updates counts, captures the
first error, fires the progress heartbeat, latches the report at
`MIN_DONE_WORDS`, and (under `RESYNC`) idles when the frame-sync goes low.

The dep-FS arming is what lets the bidirectional builds re-acquire: any
frame-sync rise arms the checker, and a false or glitch rise self-corrects
through the mid-word abort, so no startup-artifact bookkeeping is needed.
The first complete word seen only sets `pass_ready` (dropping the startup
frame); checking begins on the word after.

<<sport_rx_chan: the frame state machine and word check>>=
   always @(`ACLK_SAMPLE_EDGE aclk_global) begin
      afs_d <= afs_r;
<<sport_rx_chan: DIAG_GAPAUDIT auditor>>
<<sport_rx_chan: DIAG_STATE probe>>
<<sport_rx_chan: DIAG_FSPHASE probe>>
      if (!run && !done_aclk && !started) begin
<<sport_rx_chan: state --- idle reset>>
      end else if (!run && done_aclk) begin
<<sport_rx_chan: state --- hold the finished report>>
      end else if (RESYNC && !started && afs_r && !afs_d) begin
<<sport_rx_chan: state --- RESYNC arm on any frame-sync rise>>
      end else if (!started && afs_r && !afs_d) begin
<<sport_rx_chan: state --- plain arm>>
      end else if (!started && armed && arm_wait < 3'd3) begin
<<sport_rx_chan: state --- plain arm_wait>>
      end else if (!started && armed) begin
<<sport_rx_chan: state --- plain start>>
      end else if (RESYNC && started && !afs_r && bitpos != 5'd31) begin
<<sport_rx_chan: state --- RESYNC mid-word abort>>
      end else if (started) begin
<<sport_rx_chan: state --- running check>>
      end
   end
@

While `run` is low and the checker has neither started nor finished, the
state machine sits in idle reset: everything reloads its power-on value
so the next `run` starts from a clean seed. (`DIAG_FORCE_LIVE` keeps the
report registers live across this reset for mid-run readout.)

<<sport_rx_chan: state --- idle reset>>=
            lfsr <= PRBS31_SEED;
            afs_d <= 1'b0;
            bitpos <= 5'd0;
            started <= 1'b0;
            armed <= 1'b0;
            arm_wait <= 3'd0;
            pass_ready <= 1'b0;
            shift_word <= 32'd0;
            exp_shift_word <= 32'd0;
            wcount <= 32'd0;
            ecount <= 32'd0;
            done_aclk <= 1'b0;
            fe_seen <= 1'b0;
            fs_seen <= 1'b0;
`ifndef DIAG_FORCE_LIVE
            report_words_aclk <= 32'd0;
            report_errors_aclk <= 32'd0;
`endif
@

Once `done` is latched and `run` drops, the report is held: only the
acquire flags are cleared, so the counts and the report registers stay
put for the slow domain to read.

<<sport_rx_chan: state --- hold the finished report>>=
            started <= 1'b0;
            armed <= 1'b0;
@

The `RESYNC` builds arm on *any* frame-sync rise and start checking
immediately: this is the re-acquire entry. `pass_ready` is set right
away (the first word is consumed to seed the engine), the LFSR snapshot
`lfsr_word` is taken so a later mid-word abort can rewind, and the first
bit is shifted in.

<<sport_rx_chan: state --- RESYNC arm on any frame-sync rise>>=
            started <= 1'b1;
            pass_ready <= 1'b1;
            lfsr_word <= lfsr;
            bitpos <= 5'd1;
            shift_word <= MSB_FIRST ? {31'd0, ad0_r}
                                    : (ad0_r ? 32'h00000001 : 32'd0);
            exp_shift_word <= {31'd0, exp_bit};
            lfsr <= lfsr_next;
@

In the non-`RESYNC` builds arming is a three-step pipeline. A frame-sync
rise arms the checker and resets the settle counter:

<<sport_rx_chan: state --- plain arm>>=
            armed <= 1'b1;
            arm_wait <= 3'd0;
@

then `arm_wait` counts three stable edges before starting:

<<sport_rx_chan: state --- plain arm_wait>>=
            arm_wait <= arm_wait + 3'd1;
@

and on the fourth it starts, shifting in the first bit. The LFSR only
advances once `pass_ready` is set (the very first word is dropped as the
startup frame).

<<sport_rx_chan: state --- plain start>>=
            armed <= 1'b0;
            bitpos <= 5'd1;
            started <= 1'b1;
            shift_word <= MSB_FIRST ? {31'd0, ad0_r}
                                    : (ad0_r ? 32'h00000001 : 32'd0);
            if (pass_ready) begin
               exp_shift_word <= {31'd0, exp_bit};
               lfsr <= lfsr_next;
            end
@

If the frame-sync dips before bit 31 mid-word, the `RESYNC` builds treat
it as a lost frame: rewind the LFSR to the snapshot taken at the last
boundary and drop back to unstarted so the next rise re-arms cleanly.

<<sport_rx_chan: state --- RESYNC mid-word abort>>=
            started <= 1'b0;
            bitpos <= 5'd0;
            shift_word <= 32'd0;
            exp_shift_word <= 32'd0;
            lfsr <= lfsr_word;
@

The running check shifts in each bit, advancing the LFSR in step (once
`pass_ready`). On the word boundary (`bitpos == 31`) it either drops the
startup word or runs the per-word check; between boundaries it just
counts bits up.

<<sport_rx_chan: state --- running check>>=
            shift_word <= next_word;
            if (pass_ready) begin
               exp_shift_word <= next_exp_word;
               lfsr <= lfsr_next;
            end
`ifdef DIAG_FORCE_LIVE
            report_words_aclk <= wcount;
            report_errors_aclk <= ecount;
`endif
            if (bitpos == 5'd31) begin
               if (!pass_ready) begin
                  pass_ready <= 1'b1;
               end else begin
<<sport_rx_chan: DIAG_FIRST capture>>
<<sport_rx_chan: check --- compare and count the word>>
<<sport_rx_chan: check --- progress heartbeat>>
<<sport_rx_chan: check --- capture the first error>>
<<sport_rx_chan: check --- latch the report at MIN_DONE_WORDS>>
<<sport_rx_chan: DIAG_WORDS capture>>
               end
            bitpos <= 5'd0;
            shift_word <= 32'd0;
            exp_shift_word <= 32'd0;
            if (RESYNC && !afs_r)
               started <= 1'b0;
            if (RESYNC)
               lfsr_word <= lfsr_next;
            end else begin
               bitpos <= bitpos + 5'd1;
            end
@

The word compare bumps the word count and, on a miscompare, the error
count:

<<sport_rx_chan: check --- compare and count the word>>=
               wcount <= wcount + 32'd1;
               if (next_word_bad) begin
                  ecount <= ecount + 32'd1;
               end
@

Every `PROG_MASK`+1 words (the roughly 1 Hz boundary) the current counts
are snapshotted into the progress fields and `prog_t_r` toggled:

<<sport_rx_chan: check --- progress heartbeat>>=
               if (((wcount + 32'd1) & PROG_MASK) == 32'd0) begin
                  prog_w_r <= wcount + 32'd1;
                  prog_e_r <= ecount + (next_word_bad ? 32'd1 : 32'd0);
                  prog_t_r <= ~prog_t_r;
               end
@

The first miscompare is captured once --- its index, received, and
expected word --- with `fe_t_r` toggled to hand it to the slow domain:

<<sport_rx_chan: check --- capture the first error>>=
               if (next_word_bad && !fe_seen) begin
                  fe_seen  <= 1'b1;
                  fe_idx_r <= wcount;
                  fe_got_r <= next_word;
                  fe_exp_r <= next_exp_word;
                  fe_t_r   <= ~fe_t_r;
               end
@

When the word count reaches `MIN_DONE_WORDS` the transfer is complete:
`done_aclk` latches, the final counts are frozen into the report
registers, and `done_toggle` flips to cross the completion to `clk12`.

<<sport_rx_chan: check --- latch the report at MIN_DONE_WORDS>>=
               if (!done_aclk && (wcount + 32'd1) >= MIN_DONE_WORDS) begin
                  done_aclk <= 1'b1;
                  done_toggle <= ~done_toggle;
                  report_words_aclk <= wcount + 32'd1;
                  report_errors_aclk <= ecount + (next_word_bad ? 32'd1 : 32'd0);
               end
@

The frame-sync gap auditor, under `DIAG_GAPAUDIT`, locks the nominal
per-frame edge count (32) and the nominal frame-sync bit position on the
first frames, then records any later frame whose span or bit position
deviates as `{gap[7:0], bitpos, wcount[18:0]}`, with the live word and error
counts mirrored into the last two diag slots so a dump stands alone.

<<sport_rx_chan: DIAG_GAPAUDIT auditor>>=
`ifdef DIAG_GAPAUDIT
      if (afs_r && !afs_d) begin
         if (started && !gap_lock) begin
            gap_nominal <= gap_audit + 16'd1;
            gap_lock <= 1'b1;
         end else if (fsbit_lock == 1'b0 && gap_lock) begin
            fsbit_nominal <= bitpos;
            fsbit_lock <= 1'b1;
         end else if (gap_lock && fsbit_lock
                      && ((gap_audit + 16'd1) != gap_nominal
                          || bitpos != fsbit_nominal)
                      && diag_count < 4'd8) begin
            case (diag_count)
               4'd0: diag_word0 <= {gap_audit[7:0] + 8'd1, bitpos, wcount[18:0]};
               4'd1: diag_word1 <= {gap_audit[7:0] + 8'd1, bitpos, wcount[18:0]};
               4'd2: diag_word2 <= {gap_audit[7:0] + 8'd1, bitpos, wcount[18:0]};
               4'd3: diag_word3 <= {gap_audit[7:0] + 8'd1, bitpos, wcount[18:0]};
               4'd4: diag_word4 <= {gap_audit[7:0] + 8'd1, bitpos, wcount[18:0]};
               4'd5: diag_word5 <= {gap_audit[7:0] + 8'd1, bitpos, wcount[18:0]};
               4'd6: diag_word6 <= {gap_audit[7:0] + 8'd1, bitpos, wcount[18:0]};
               4'd7: diag_word7 <= {gap_audit[7:0] + 8'd1, bitpos, wcount[18:0]};
               4'd8: diag_word8 <= {gap_audit[7:0] + 8'd1, bitpos, wcount[18:0]};
               4'd9: diag_word9 <= {gap_audit[7:0] + 8'd1, bitpos, wcount[18:0]};
               default: begin end
            endcase
            diag_count <= diag_count + 4'd1;
         end
         gap_audit <= 16'd0;
      end else begin
         gap_audit <= gap_audit + 16'd1;
      end
      diag_word8 <= wcount;
      diag_word9 <= ecount;
`endif
@

`DIAG_STATE` mirrors the acquire flags into `fe_idx_r` for inspection:

<<sport_rx_chan: DIAG_STATE probe>>=
`ifdef DIAG_STATE
      fe_idx_r <= {28'd0, fs_seen, armed, started, pass_ready};
`endif
@

`DIAG_FSPHASE` records the gap between frame-sync rises and the bit
position at each rise into the diag slots and the progress fields, then
arms `done` after ten captures so the snapshot is reported:

<<sport_rx_chan: DIAG_FSPHASE probe>>=
`ifdef DIAG_FSPHASE
      fs_gap <= fs_gap + 16'd1;
      if (run && afs_r && !afs_d) begin
         fs_gap <= 16'd0;
         prog_w_r <= {16'd0, fs_gap};
         prog_e_r <= {prog_e_r[31:16] + 16'd1, 5'd0, started, 5'd0, bitpos};
         if (diag_count < 4'd10) begin
            case (diag_count)
               4'd0: diag_word0 <= {fs_gap, 5'd0, started, 5'd0, bitpos};
               4'd1: diag_word1 <= {fs_gap, 5'd0, started, 5'd0, bitpos};
               4'd2: diag_word2 <= {fs_gap, 5'd0, started, 5'd0, bitpos};
               4'd3: diag_word3 <= {fs_gap, 5'd0, started, 5'd0, bitpos};
               4'd4: diag_word4 <= {fs_gap, 5'd0, started, 5'd0, bitpos};
               4'd5: diag_word5 <= {fs_gap, 5'd0, started, 5'd0, bitpos};
               4'd6: diag_word6 <= {fs_gap, 5'd0, started, 5'd0, bitpos};
               4'd7: diag_word7 <= {fs_gap, 5'd0, started, 5'd0, bitpos};
               4'd8: diag_word8 <= {fs_gap, 5'd0, started, 5'd0, bitpos};
               4'd9: diag_word9 <= {fs_gap, 5'd0, started, 5'd0, bitpos};
               default: begin end
            endcase
            diag_count <= diag_count + 4'd1;
            if (!done_aclk) begin
               done_aclk <= 1'b1;
               done_toggle <= ~done_toggle;
            end
         end
      end
`endif
@

`DIAG_FIRST` latches the first received/expected word and its index ---
under `DIAG_MISMATCH` only on the first mismatching word:

<<sport_rx_chan: DIAG_FIRST capture>>=
`ifdef DIAG_FIRST
`ifdef DIAG_MISMATCH
               if (!first_seen && next_word_bad) begin
`else
               if (!first_seen) begin
`endif
                  first_rx_r <= next_word;
                  first_exp_r <= next_exp_word;
                  first_idx_r <= wcount;
                  first_seen <= 1'b1;
               end
`endif
@

`DIAG_WORDS` captures the first ten checked words (past `DIAG_BASE`) into
the diag slots, disabled when the FS-phase or gap auditors own those slots:

<<sport_rx_chan: DIAG_WORDS capture>>=
`ifdef DIAG_WORDS
`ifndef DIAG_FSPHASE
`ifndef DIAG_GAPAUDIT
               if (wcount >= DIAG_BASE && diag_count < 4'd10) begin
                  case (diag_count)
                     4'd0: diag_word0 <= next_word;
                     4'd1: diag_word1 <= next_word;
                     4'd2: diag_word2 <= next_word;
                     4'd3: diag_word3 <= next_word;
                     4'd4: diag_word4 <= next_word;
                     4'd5: diag_word5 <= next_word;
                     4'd6: diag_word6 <= next_word;
                     4'd7: diag_word7 <= next_word;
                     4'd8: diag_word8 <= next_word;
                     4'd9: diag_word9 <= next_word;
                     default: begin end
                  endcase
                  diag_count <= diag_count + 4'd1;
               end
`endif
`endif
`endif
@

The aclk-domain `done_toggle` and a free-running `aclk_toggle` are carried
into the `clk12` domain through three-stage synchronizers. `aclk_active` is
true while the forwarded clock is toggling; when it stops for `IDLE_THRESH`
the link is considered idle. The completed report crosses on a
`done_pending`/`done_wait` handshake: when `done_toggle` flips, wait
`8'hff` `clk12` ticks for the aclk-domain count registers to be stable, then
latch `report_words`/`report_errors` and raise `done_r`. `DIAG_RAW` and
`DIAG_FORCE_REPORT` are alternative report triggers --- a raw edge count, or
a forced report after about 10 s --- used during bring-up.

<<sport_rx_chan: cross to clk12 and detect idle>>=
   reg aclk_toggle = 1'b0;
   always @(posedge aclk_global) aclk_toggle <= ~aclk_toggle;

`ifdef DIAG_RAW
   reg [31:0] raw_edges = 32'd0;
   always @(posedge aclk_global) raw_edges <= raw_edges + 32'd1;
`endif

   reg [2:0] tog_sync = 3'b000;
   always @(posedge clk12) tog_sync <= {tog_sync[1:0], aclk_toggle};
   wire aclk_active = (tog_sync[2] ^ tog_sync[1]);

   reg [16:0] idle_cnt = 17'd0;
   localparam IDLE_THRESH = 17'd60000;
   reg done_r = 1'b0;

   reg [31:0] report_words = 32'd0;
   reg [31:0] report_errors = 32'd0;
   reg [2:0] done_sync = 3'b000;
   reg        done_pending = 1'b0;
   reg [7:0]  done_wait = 8'd0;
`ifdef DIAG_RAW
 `define DIAG_FORCE_CNT
`elsif DIAG_FORCE_REPORT
 `define DIAG_FORCE_CNT
`endif
`ifdef DIAG_FORCE_CNT
   reg [31:0] force_cnt = 32'd0;
`endif
   always @(posedge clk12) begin
      done_sync <= {done_sync[1:0], done_toggle};
`ifdef DIAG_RAW
      report_words <= raw_edges;
      report_errors <= 32'd0;
`elsif DIAG_FORCE_REPORT
      if (force_cnt < 32'd120000000) force_cnt <= force_cnt + 32'd1;
      if (!done_r && force_cnt == 32'd119999999) begin
         report_words <= report_words_aclk;
         report_errors <= report_errors_aclk;
         done_r <= 1'b1;
      end
`endif
      if (aclk_active) begin
         idle_cnt <= 17'd0;
      end else if (idle_cnt < IDLE_THRESH) begin
         idle_cnt <= idle_cnt + 17'd1;
      end
`ifdef DIAG_RAW
      if (force_cnt < 32'd120000000) force_cnt <= force_cnt + 32'd1;
      if (!done_r && force_cnt == 32'd119999999) done_r <= 1'b1;
`elsif DIAG_FORCE_REPORT
`else
      if (!done_r && !done_pending && (done_sync[2] ^ done_sync[1])) begin
         done_pending <= 1'b1;
         done_wait <= 8'd0;
      end else if (done_pending) begin
         done_wait <= done_wait + 8'd1;
         if (done_wait == 8'hff) begin
            report_words <= report_words_aclk;
            report_errors <= report_errors_aclk;
            done_r <= 1'b1;
            done_pending <= 1'b0;
         end
      end
`endif
   end
@

The outputs expose the latched report and, when their diagnostics are
enabled, the first-word and diag captures; otherwise those tie off to zero.

<<sport_rx_chan: drive the outputs>>=
   assign words = report_words;
   assign errors = report_errors;
`ifdef DIAG_FIRST
   assign first_rx = first_rx_r;
   assign first_exp = first_exp_r;
   assign first_idx = first_idx_r;
`else
   assign first_rx = 32'd0;
   assign first_exp = 32'd0;
   assign first_idx = 32'd0;
`endif
`ifdef DIAG_WORDS
   assign diag0 = diag_word0;
   assign diag1 = diag_word1;
   assign diag2 = diag_word2;
   assign diag3 = diag_word3;
   assign diag4 = diag_word4;
   assign diag5 = diag_word5;
   assign diag6 = diag_word6;
   assign diag7 = diag_word7;
   assign diag8 = diag_word8;
   assign diag9 = diag_word9;
`else
   assign diag0 = 32'd0;
   assign diag1 = 32'd0;
   assign diag2 = 32'd0;
   assign diag3 = 32'd0;
   assign diag4 = 32'd0;
   assign diag5 = 32'd0;
   assign diag6 = 32'd0;
   assign diag7 = 32'd0;
   assign diag8 = 32'd0;
   assign diag9 = 32'd0;
`endif
   assign done = done_r;
@

==== The N-lane wrapper

`sport_rx` instantiates `N` channels, gates `run`, registers the lane pins
in the IO cells, aggregates the per-lane results, and drives the UART
report on the `tx` pad. The parameters select the verification policy:
`N` is the lane count; `MIN_DONE_WORDS` how many good words make a
completed transfer; `RESYNC` is passed down to each channel to enable the
re-acquiring frame state machine. `REPORT_LANE0` together with
`REPORT_IDX` narrow the done gate and the report to one lane (`REPORT_IDX`)
instead of folding all `N` --- the single-lane proof --- and `REPORT_IDX`
also picks which lane the progress/first-error lines watch. `led` toggles
once per heartbeat line, so it blinks at about 0.5 Hz while data flows and
holds steady when idle.

<<sport_rx: the N-lane wrapper>>=
module sport_rx #(
      parameter N = 2,
      parameter MIN_DONE_WORDS = 32'h00b00000,
      parameter REPORT_LANE0 = 0,
      parameter REPORT_IDX = 0,
      parameter RESYNC = 0
   )(
      input        clk12,
      input  [N-1:0] aclk_in,
      input  [N-1:0] ad0_in,
      input  [N-1:0] afs_in,
      input        run,
      inout        tx,
      output reg   led
   );

   initial led = 1'b0;

   <<sport_rx: per-lane result bus>>
   <<sport_rx: the run-seen-low gate>>
   <<sport_rx: register the lane pins in the IO cells>>
   <<sport_rx: instantiate the lanes>>
   <<sport_rx: hex and diag helper functions>>
   <<sport_rx: progress and first-error report plumbing>>
   <<sport_rx: the live progress and error character emitters>>
   <<sport_rx: loop-free lane aggregation>>
   <<sport_rx: the final report character emitter>>
   <<sport_rx: the send FSM>>
   <<sport_rx: the UART and tx pad>>
endmodule
@

Each per-lane output is an `N`-wide unpacked array tapped by the lanes:

<<sport_rx: per-lane result bus>>=
   wire [31:0] words [0:N-1];
   wire [31:0] errors [0:N-1];
   wire [31:0] first_rx [0:N-1];
   wire [31:0] first_exp [0:N-1];
   wire [31:0] first_idx [0:N-1];
   wire [31:0] diag0 [0:N-1];
   wire [31:0] diag1 [0:N-1];
   wire [31:0] diag2 [0:N-1];
   wire [31:0] diag3 [0:N-1];
   wire [31:0] diag4 [0:N-1];
   wire [31:0] diag5 [0:N-1];
   wire [31:0] diag6 [0:N-1];
   wire [31:0] diag7 [0:N-1];
   wire [31:0] diag8 [0:N-1];
   wire [31:0] diag9 [0:N-1];
   wire        done [0:N-1];
   wire [31:0] prog_w [0:N-1];
   wire [31:0] prog_e [0:N-1];
   wire        prog_t [0:N-1];
   wire [31:0] fe_idx [0:N-1];
   wire [31:0] fe_got [0:N-1];
   wire [31:0] fe_exp [0:N-1];
   wire        fe_t [0:N-1];
@

Between FPGA configuration and DSP firmware start, `run` (R10) floats high
while the DSP's SPI boot clocks MHz-rate noise into the lanes. A
floating-armed receiver could cross a small `MIN_DONE` during boot and fire
its one-shot report into the boot bus. So `run` is honoured only after it
has genuinely been seen low (the firmware holds it low 60 ms at start);
zero-init means "not yet seen low", which iCE40 silicon honours. The
`run_gated` signal carries that to the lanes.

<<sport_rx: the run-seen-low gate>>=
   reg [2:0] run_sync = 3'b000;
   reg       run_seen_low = 1'b0;
   always @(posedge clk12) begin
      run_sync <= {run_sync[1:0], run};
      if (run_sync[2:1] == 2'b00)
         run_seen_low <= 1'b1;
   end
   wire run_gated = run & run_seen_low;
@

Each lane's `ad0`/`afs` are registered in the IO cells, clocked by the
lane's own forwarded clock, so the pin-to-flop timing is fixed in silicon
rather than varying with placement and routing (a fabric-routed input flop
can sit marginally close to the sample edge and silently corrupt the
stream). `NO_IOREG` bypasses the registers; `SAMPLE_NEG` flips the IO flop
to the negative edge; and `EYE_DELAY` routes the IO-FF clock through a kept
LUT (taking fabric routing, +2..4 ns) to slide the sample point into the
data eye.

<<sport_rx: register the lane pins in the IO cells>>=
   wire [N-1:0] ad0_reg, afs_reg;
`ifdef NO_IOREG
   assign ad0_reg = ad0_in;
   assign afs_reg = afs_in;
`else
`ifdef SAMPLE_NEG
   localparam IO_NEG_TRIGGER = 1'b1;
`else
   localparam IO_NEG_TRIGGER = 1'b0;
`endif
   genvar io_i;
   generate
      for (io_i = 0; io_i < N; io_i = io_i + 1) begin : ioregs
`ifdef EYE_DELAY
         <<sport_rx: input flops on the eye-delayed clock>>
`else
         <<sport_rx: input flops on the direct clock>>
`endif
      end
   endgenerate
`endif
@

The two arms are the same pair of `SB_IO` input flops; the only
difference is which net feeds `.INPUT_CLK`. The default arm clocks each
flop straight off the lane's forwarded clock `aclk_in[io_i]`:

<<sport_rx: input flops on the direct clock>>=
SB_IO #(.PIN_TYPE(6'b000000), .NEG_TRIGGER(IO_NEG_TRIGGER)) ad0_iob (
   .PACKAGE_PIN(ad0_in[io_i]),
   .INPUT_CLK(aclk_in[io_i]),
   .D_IN_0(ad0_reg[io_i]));
SB_IO #(.PIN_TYPE(6'b000000), .NEG_TRIGGER(IO_NEG_TRIGGER)) afs_iob (
   .PACKAGE_PIN(afs_in[io_i]),
   .INPUT_CLK(aclk_in[io_i]),
   .D_IN_0(afs_reg[io_i]));
@

The `EYE_DELAY` arm is identical except the flops are clocked by
`aclk_dly` --- the forwarded clock routed through a kept `SB_LUT4`. The
LUT is configured `LUT_INIT=16'haaaa`, which is the truth table of "copy
`I0`": a pure pass-through whose only purpose is the +2..4 ns of fabric
routing delay it forces in, sliding the sample point deeper into the
data eye. The `(* keep *)` attribute stops synthesis from optimizing the
pass-through LUT away.

<<sport_rx: input flops on the eye-delayed clock>>=
(* keep *) wire aclk_dly;
SB_LUT4 #(.LUT_INIT(16'haaaa)) eye_dly (
   .O(aclk_dly), .I0(aclk_in[io_i]),
   .I1(1'b0), .I2(1'b0), .I3(1'b0));
SB_IO #(.PIN_TYPE(6'b000000), .NEG_TRIGGER(IO_NEG_TRIGGER)) ad0_iob (
   .PACKAGE_PIN(ad0_in[io_i]),
   .INPUT_CLK(aclk_dly),
   .D_IN_0(ad0_reg[io_i]));
SB_IO #(.PIN_TYPE(6'b000000), .NEG_TRIGGER(IO_NEG_TRIGGER)) afs_iob (
   .PACKAGE_PIN(afs_in[io_i]),
   .INPUT_CLK(aclk_dly),
   .D_IN_0(afs_reg[io_i]));
@

The lane `generate` instantiates one `sport_rx_chan` per lane, fed the
registered pins and `run_gated`, and fans its results back onto the result
bus.

<<sport_rx: instantiate the lanes>>=
   genvar lane_i;
   generate
      for (lane_i = 0; lane_i < N; lane_i = lane_i + 1) begin : lanes
         sport_rx_chan #(.MIN_DONE_WORDS(MIN_DONE_WORDS), .RESYNC(RESYNC)) lane (
            .clk12(clk12),
            .run(run_gated),
            .aclk_in(aclk_in[lane_i]),
            .ad0_in(ad0_reg[lane_i]),
            .afs_in(afs_reg[lane_i]),
            .words(words[lane_i]),
            .errors(errors[lane_i]),
            .first_rx(first_rx[lane_i]),
            .first_exp(first_exp[lane_i]),
            .first_idx(first_idx[lane_i]),
            .diag0(diag0[lane_i]),
            .diag1(diag1[lane_i]),
            .diag2(diag2[lane_i]),
            .diag3(diag3[lane_i]),
            .diag4(diag4[lane_i]),
            .diag5(diag5[lane_i]),
            .diag6(diag6[lane_i]),
            .diag7(diag7[lane_i]),
            .diag8(diag8[lane_i]),
            .diag9(diag9[lane_i]),
            .done(done[lane_i]),
            .prog_w(prog_w[lane_i]),
            .prog_e(prog_e[lane_i]),
            .prog_t(prog_t[lane_i]),
            .fe_idx_o(fe_idx[lane_i]),
            .fe_got_o(fe_got[lane_i]),
            .fe_exp_o(fe_exp[lane_i]),
            .fe_t(fe_t[lane_i])
         );
      end
   endgenerate
@

The UART message is assembled character by character by a set of pure
functions over the latched snapshots. `hex_digit` and `hex_word_digit`
render a nibble and a chosen nibble of a 32-bit word; `diag_word` and
`diag_prefix_char` serve the `DIAG_WORDS` dump (the prefix spells
`sport_rx_words`).

<<sport_rx: hex and diag helper functions>>=
   function [7:0] hex_digit(input [3:0] nibble);
      begin
         hex_digit = (nibble < 4'd10) ? ("0" + nibble) : ("a" + (nibble - 4'd10));
      end
   endfunction

   function [31:0] diag_word(input [3:0] which);
      begin
         case (which)
            4'd0: diag_word = diag0[REPORT_IDX];
            4'd1: diag_word = diag1[REPORT_IDX];
            4'd2: diag_word = diag2[REPORT_IDX];
            4'd3: diag_word = diag3[REPORT_IDX];
            4'd4: diag_word = diag4[REPORT_IDX];
            4'd5: diag_word = diag5[REPORT_IDX];
            4'd6: diag_word = diag6[REPORT_IDX];
            4'd7: diag_word = diag7[REPORT_IDX];
            4'd8: diag_word = diag8[REPORT_IDX];
            default: diag_word = diag9[REPORT_IDX];
         endcase
      end
   endfunction

   function [7:0] diag_prefix_char(input [3:0] idx);
      begin
         case (idx)
            4'd0: diag_prefix_char = "s"; 4'd1: diag_prefix_char = "p";
            4'd2: diag_prefix_char = "o"; 4'd3: diag_prefix_char = "r";
            4'd4: diag_prefix_char = "t"; 4'd5: diag_prefix_char = "_";
            4'd6: diag_prefix_char = "r"; 4'd7: diag_prefix_char = "x";
            4'd8: diag_prefix_char = "_"; 4'd9: diag_prefix_char = "w";
            4'd10: diag_prefix_char = "o"; 4'd11: diag_prefix_char = "r";
            4'd12: diag_prefix_char = "d"; 4'd13: diag_prefix_char = "s";
            default: diag_prefix_char = " ";
         endcase
      end
   endfunction

   function [7:0] hex_word_digit(input [31:0] word, input [2:0] digit);
      begin
         case (digit)
            3'd0: hex_word_digit = hex_digit(word[31:28]);
            3'd1: hex_word_digit = hex_digit(word[27:24]);
            3'd2: hex_word_digit = hex_digit(word[23:20]);
            3'd3: hex_word_digit = hex_digit(word[19:16]);
            3'd4: hex_word_digit = hex_digit(word[15:12]);
            3'd5: hex_word_digit = hex_digit(word[11:8]);
            3'd6: hex_word_digit = hex_digit(word[7:4]);
            default: hex_word_digit = hex_digit(word[3:0]);
         endcase
      end
   endfunction
@

Two extra lines can be emitted besides the final report: a live progress
line `rx w=<hex> e=<hex>` roughly once per second, and a one-shot first-error
line `ERR w=<idx> got=<hex> exp=<hex>`, both built from the `REPORT_IDX`
lane's aclk-domain snapshots. The progress/first-error toggles are
synchronized into `clk12` (`pt_sync`/`fet_sync`) and set the `prog_pend` and
`fe_pend` flags; `msg_kind` selects which message the send FSM is emitting.
`DIAG_STATE` repurposes this path to print a deferred state dump and a
periodic probe instead.

<<sport_rx: progress and first-error report plumbing>>=
`ifdef DIAG_WORDS
   localparam PROG_EN = 1'b0;
`else
   localparam PROG_EN = 1'b1;
`endif
   reg [2:0]  pt_sync = 3'b000;
   reg [2:0]  fet_sync = 3'b000;
   reg        prog_pend = 1'b0;
   reg        fe_pend = 1'b0;
   reg [31:0] pw_latch = 32'd0;
   reg [31:0] pe_latch = 32'd0;
   reg [31:0] fi_latch = 32'd0;
   reg [31:0] fg_latch = 32'd0;
   reg [31:0] fx_latch = 32'd0;
   reg [1:0]  msg_kind = 2'd0;
`ifdef DIAG_STATE
   reg [25:0] diag_timer = 26'd0;
   reg        diag_go = 1'b0;
   wire [31:0] fe_probe = fe_idx[REPORT_IDX];
`endif
@

`prog_char` renders the progress line and `err_char` the first-error line,
each placing the hex fields at fixed column ranges and the literal label
characters elsewhere. The column arithmetic is the same trick in every
emitter: a character index that falls inside a hex field is mapped to a
nibble by subtracting the field's start column and feeding the remainder
(masked to three bits) to `hex_word_digit`, while indices outside any
field select a literal byte from a `case`. The per-field offsets (the
`- 3'd5`, `- 3'd6`, `- 3'd2` constants and the like) are just each
field's start column modulo eight, chosen so digit 0 is the most
significant nibble.

<<sport_rx: the live progress and error character emitters>>=
   function [7:0] prog_char(input [7:0] idx);
      begin
         if (idx >= 8'd5 && idx <= 8'd12) begin
            prog_char = hex_word_digit(pw_latch, idx[2:0] - 3'd5);
         end else if (idx >= 8'd16 && idx <= 8'd23) begin
            prog_char = hex_word_digit(pe_latch, idx[2:0]);
         end else begin
            case (idx)
               8'd0: prog_char = "r"; 8'd1: prog_char = "x";
               8'd2: prog_char = " "; 8'd3: prog_char = "w";
               8'd4: prog_char = "="; 8'd13: prog_char = " ";
               8'd14: prog_char = "e"; 8'd15: prog_char = "=";
               8'd24: prog_char = "\r";
               default: prog_char = "\n";
            endcase
         end
      end
   endfunction

   function [7:0] err_char(input [7:0] idx);
      begin
         if (idx >= 8'd6 && idx <= 8'd13) begin
            err_char = hex_word_digit(fi_latch, idx[2:0] - 3'd6);
         end else if (idx >= 8'd19 && idx <= 8'd26) begin
            err_char = hex_word_digit(fg_latch, idx[2:0] - 3'd3);
         end else if (idx >= 8'd32 && idx <= 8'd39) begin
            err_char = hex_word_digit(fx_latch, idx[2:0]);
         end else begin
            case (idx)
               8'd0: err_char = "E"; 8'd1: err_char = "R";
               8'd2: err_char = "R"; 8'd3: err_char = " ";
               8'd4: err_char = "w"; 8'd5: err_char = "=";
               8'd14: err_char = " "; 8'd15: err_char = "g";
               8'd16: err_char = "o"; 8'd17: err_char = "t";
               8'd18: err_char = "="; 8'd27: err_char = " ";
               8'd28: err_char = "e"; 8'd29: err_char = "x";
               8'd30: err_char = "p"; 8'd31: err_char = "=";
               8'd40: err_char = "\r";
               default: err_char = "\n";
            endcase
         end
      end
   endfunction
@

The final report needs the minimum word count across all lanes and the
total error count. Since iCE40 has no good way to fold an unpacked array in
one expression, the fold is unrolled into a chain of continuous assigns:
`minw_ch[i]` is the running minimum and `tote_ch[i]` the running error sum,
with `min_words_c`/`tot_err_c` the last links. `all_done_w` is the global
done --- a single lane under `REPORT_LANE0`, otherwise the AND of all lanes.

<<sport_rx: loop-free lane aggregation>>=
   reg tx_oe = 1'b0;
   reg reported = 1'b0;
   reg sending = 1'b0;
   reg pre_wait = 1'b0;
   reg [14:0] pre_cnt = 15'd0;
   reg [7:0] send_idx = 8'd0;
   reg [3:0] diag_send_word = 4'd0;
   reg [3:0] diag_send_digit = 4'd0;
   reg send_start = 1'b0;
   reg [7:0] send_data = 8'd0;
   wire uart_busy;
   wire uart_tx_line;

   wire [N-1:0]  done_vec;
   wire [31:0]   minw_ch [0:N-1];
   wire [31:0]   tote_ch [0:N-1];
   assign done_vec[0] = done[0];
   assign minw_ch[0] = words[0];
   assign tote_ch[0] = errors[0];
   genvar agg_i;
   generate
      for (agg_i = 1; agg_i < N; agg_i = agg_i + 1) begin : agg
         assign done_vec[agg_i] = done[agg_i];
         assign minw_ch[agg_i] = (words[agg_i] < minw_ch[agg_i-1])
                                 ? words[agg_i] : minw_ch[agg_i-1];
         assign tote_ch[agg_i] = tote_ch[agg_i-1] + errors[agg_i];
      end
   endgenerate
   wire [31:0] min_words_c = minw_ch[N-1];
   wire [31:0] tot_err_c  = tote_ch[N-1];
   wire        all_done_w = REPORT_LANE0 ? done[REPORT_IDX] : (&done_vec);

   reg [31:0] w0_latch = 32'd0;
   reg [31:0] et_latch = 32'd0;
@

`report_char` renders the full report line --- the `sport_rx
lanes=<N> per_ch_words_hex=<hex> errors_hex=<hex>` prefix followed by
`PASS` when the latched error count is zero and the word count reached
`MIN_DONE_WORDS`, else `FAIL`.

<<sport_rx: the final report character emitter>>=
   function [7:0] report_char(input [7:0] idx);
      begin
         if (idx >= 8'd34 && idx <= 8'd41) begin
            report_char = hex_word_digit(w0_latch, idx[2:0] - 3'd2);
         end else if (idx >= 8'd54 && idx <= 8'd61) begin
            report_char = hex_word_digit(et_latch, idx[2:0] - 3'd6);
         end else if (idx >= 8'd63 && idx <= 8'd66) begin
            if (et_latch == 32'd0 && w0_latch >= MIN_DONE_WORDS) begin
               case (idx)
                  8'd63: report_char = "P";
                  8'd64: report_char = "A";
                  8'd65: report_char = "S";
                  default: report_char = "S";
               endcase
            end else begin
               case (idx)
                  8'd63: report_char = "F";
                  8'd64: report_char = "A";
                  8'd65: report_char = "I";
                  default: report_char = "L";
               endcase
            end
         end else begin
            case (idx)
               8'd0: report_char = "s"; 8'd1: report_char = "p";
               8'd2: report_char = "o"; 8'd3: report_char = "r";
               8'd4: report_char = "t"; 8'd5: report_char = "_";
               8'd6: report_char = "r"; 8'd7: report_char = "x";
               8'd8: report_char = " "; 8'd9: report_char = "l";
               8'd10: report_char = "a"; 8'd11: report_char = "n";
               8'd12: report_char = "e"; 8'd13: report_char = "s";
               8'd14: report_char = "="; 8'd15: report_char = "0" + N;
               8'd16: report_char = " "; 8'd17: report_char = "p";
               8'd18: report_char = "e"; 8'd19: report_char = "r";
               8'd20: report_char = "_"; 8'd21: report_char = "c";
               8'd22: report_char = "h"; 8'd23: report_char = "_";
               8'd24: report_char = "w"; 8'd25: report_char = "o";
               8'd26: report_char = "r"; 8'd27: report_char = "d";
               8'd28: report_char = "s"; 8'd29: report_char = "_";
               8'd30: report_char = "h"; 8'd31: report_char = "e";
               8'd32: report_char = "x"; 8'd33: report_char = "=";
               8'd42: report_char = " "; 8'd43: report_char = "e";
               8'd44: report_char = "r"; 8'd45: report_char = "r";
               8'd46: report_char = "o"; 8'd47: report_char = "r";
               8'd48: report_char = "s"; 8'd49: report_char = "_";
               8'd50: report_char = "h"; 8'd51: report_char = "e";
               8'd52: report_char = "x"; 8'd53: report_char = "=";
               8'd62: report_char = " "; 8'd67: report_char = "\r";
               default: report_char = "\n";
            endcase
         end
      end
   endfunction
@

The send FSM is one `clk12` block. It pulses `send_start` low each cycle,
synchronizes the progress and first-error toggles, and then, in priority
order, kicks off whichever message is due: a first-error line, a progress
line (only while data still flows --- `!all_done_w`), or the final report
once `all_done_w` (single-lane or all-lane). Each kick raises `tx_oe`,
arms a `pre_wait` settling delay, and selects `msg_kind`; the progress kick
also toggles `led`. After `pre_wait` the FSM walks `send_idx` through the
chosen character emitter, handing each byte to the UART when it is not busy.
(Under `DIAG_STATE` the first-error arm is compiled out --- its guard
becomes `if (1'b0)` --- because that diagnostic build repurposes the
progress line for a state dump and never emits the normal first-error
line.) The UART pad is driven only once there is something to say:
B12 shares a net with the DSP boot bus, so driving it from configuration
would corrupt the SPI boot (SYS_FAULT) --- the first print happens long
after boot.

<<sport_rx: the send FSM>>=
   always @(posedge clk12) begin
<<sport_rx send: synchronize the toggles and set the pending flags>>
`ifdef DIAG_STATE
      if (1'b0) begin
`else
      if (PROG_EN && !sending && !pre_wait && fe_pend && !reported) begin
`endif
<<sport_rx send: kick the first-error line>>
      end else if (PROG_EN && !sending && !pre_wait && prog_pend
`ifdef DIAG_STATE
                   ) begin
`else
                   && !reported && !all_done_w) begin
`endif
<<sport_rx send: kick the progress line>>
      end else if (REPORT_LANE0 && !reported && all_done_w
                   && !sending && !pre_wait) begin
<<sport_rx send: kick the single-lane final report>>
      end else if (!REPORT_LANE0 && !reported && all_done_w
                   && !sending && !pre_wait) begin
<<sport_rx send: kick the all-lane final report>>
      end else if (pre_wait) begin
<<sport_rx send: the pre-wait settling delay>>
      end else if (sending && !uart_busy && !send_start) begin
<<sport_rx send: walk the chosen message out byte by byte>>
      end
   end
@

Each cycle the FSM clears the one-shot `send_start`, advances the
progress and first-error toggle synchronizers, and raises `prog_pend` /
`fe_pend` when a toggle is seen. (Under `DIAG_STATE` a periodic timer
also raises `prog_pend` so the state dump reprints on its own.)

<<sport_rx send: synchronize the toggles and set the pending flags>>=
      send_start <= 1'b0;
      pt_sync <= {pt_sync[1:0], prog_t[REPORT_IDX]};
      fet_sync <= {fet_sync[1:0], fe_t[REPORT_IDX]};
      if (pt_sync[2] ^ pt_sync[1])
         prog_pend <= 1'b1;
`ifdef DIAG_STATE
      diag_timer <= diag_timer + 26'd1;
      if (diag_timer == 26'd0)
         diag_go <= 1'b1;
      if (diag_go && diag_timer[22:0] == 23'd0)
         prog_pend <= 1'b1;
`endif
      if (fet_sync[2] ^ fet_sync[1])
         fe_pend <= 1'b1;
@

Highest priority: a pending first error. Latch its three fields, select
the `ERR` message (`msg_kind` 2), and arm the pre-wait.

<<sport_rx send: kick the first-error line>>=
         fi_latch <= fe_idx[REPORT_IDX];
         fg_latch <= fe_got[REPORT_IDX];
         fx_latch <= fe_exp[REPORT_IDX];
         msg_kind <= 2'd2;
         send_idx <= 8'd0;
         pre_wait <= 1'b1;
         pre_cnt <= 15'd0;
         tx_oe <= 1'b1;
         fe_pend <= 1'b0;
@

Next: a pending progress tick, but only while data still flows
(`!all_done_w`). Latch the live word/error counts, select the progress
message (`msg_kind` 1), and toggle `led`. (`DIAG_STATE` reroutes this
arm to dump acquire state instead of counts: the latched word packs a
`0x6a` (`'j'`) tag in the high byte, then `run`, the per-lane `done`
bits, and the `all_done_w`/`reported`/`sending`/`pre_wait`/`prog_pend`/
`fe_pend` flags in the low bits, so a single hex line shows where the
send FSM is stuck.)

<<sport_rx send: kick the progress line>>=
`ifdef DIAG_STATE
`ifdef DIAG_FSPHASE
         pw_latch <= prog_w[REPORT_IDX];
`else
         pw_latch <= words[REPORT_IDX];
`endif
`ifdef DIAG_FSPHASE
         pe_latch <= prog_e[REPORT_IDX];
`else
         pe_latch <= {8'h6a, 3'd0, run, 2'd0, done[0],
                      done[N > 1 ? 1 : 0], 6'd0, all_done_w, reported,
                      sending, pre_wait, prog_pend, fe_pend, 1'b0,
                      1'b0};
`endif
`else
         pw_latch <= prog_w[REPORT_IDX];
         pe_latch <= prog_e[REPORT_IDX];
`endif
         msg_kind <= 2'd1;
         send_idx <= 8'd0;
         pre_wait <= 1'b1;
         pre_cnt <= 15'd0;
         tx_oe <= 1'b1;
         prog_pend <= 1'b0;
         led <= ~led;
@

The single-lane (`REPORT_LANE0`) final report, fired once `all_done_w`:
latch lane `REPORT_IDX`'s word/error counts (or, under `DIAG_FIRST`, the
first-word capture), select the report message (`msg_kind` 0), and mark
`reported`.

<<sport_rx send: kick the single-lane final report>>=
`ifdef DIAG_FIRST
`ifdef DIAG_INDEX
         w0_latch <= first_idx[REPORT_IDX];
         et_latch <= first_rx[REPORT_IDX];
`else
         w0_latch <= first_exp[REPORT_IDX];
         et_latch <= first_rx[REPORT_IDX];
`endif
`else
         w0_latch <= words[REPORT_IDX];
         et_latch <= errors[REPORT_IDX];
`endif
         pre_wait <= 1'b1;
         pre_cnt <= 15'd0;
         send_idx <= 8'd0;
         msg_kind <= 2'd0;
         tx_oe <= 1'b1;
         diag_send_word <= 4'd0;
         diag_send_digit <= 4'd0;
         reported <= 1'b1;
@

The all-lane final report is the same kick but latches the folded
minimum word count and total error count from the aggregation chain.

<<sport_rx send: kick the all-lane final report>>=
         w0_latch <= min_words_c;
         et_latch <= tot_err_c;
         pre_wait <= 1'b1;
         pre_cnt <= 15'd0;
         send_idx <= 8'd0;
         msg_kind <= 2'd0;
         tx_oe <= 1'b1;
         diag_send_word <= 4'd0;
         diag_send_digit <= 4'd0;
         reported <= 1'b1;
@

After a kick, `pre_wait` holds 12000 `clk12` ticks (about 1 ms) so the
`tx` pad and the aclk-domain snapshots settle before the first byte goes
out; then it hands off to `sending`.

<<sport_rx send: the pre-wait settling delay>>=
         if (pre_cnt < 15'd12000) begin
            pre_cnt <= pre_cnt + 15'd1;
         end else begin
            pre_wait <= 1'b0;
            sending <= 1'b1;
            tx_oe <= 1'b1;
         end
@

While sending, each idle UART cycle emits the next character of the
selected message from its emitter function, advancing `send_idx` until
the message is done. (The `DIAG_WORDS` build emits the ten-word diag dump
instead of the normal three message kinds.)

<<sport_rx send: walk the chosen message out byte by byte>>=
`ifdef DIAG_WORDS
         if (send_idx < 8'd15) begin
            send_data <= diag_prefix_char(send_idx[3:0]);
            send_start <= 1'b1;
            send_idx <= send_idx + 8'd1;
         end else if (diag_send_word < 4'd10) begin
            if (diag_send_digit < 4'd8) begin
               send_data <= hex_word_digit(diag_word(diag_send_word),
                                           diag_send_digit[2:0]);
               diag_send_digit <= diag_send_digit + 4'd1;
            end else begin
               send_data <= " ";
               diag_send_digit <= 4'd0;
               diag_send_word <= diag_send_word + 4'd1;
            end
            send_start <= 1'b1;
         end else if (send_idx == 8'd15) begin
            send_data <= "\r";
            send_start <= 1'b1;
            send_idx <= 8'd16;
         end else if (send_idx == 8'd16) begin
            send_data <= "\n";
            send_start <= 1'b1;
            send_idx <= 8'd17;
         end else begin
            sending <= 1'b0;
         end
`else
         if (msg_kind == 2'd1) begin
            if (send_idx < 8'd26) begin
               send_data <= prog_char(send_idx);
               send_start <= 1'b1;
               send_idx <= send_idx + 8'd1;
            end else begin
               sending <= 1'b0;
            end
         end else if (msg_kind == 2'd2) begin
            if (send_idx < 8'd42) begin
               send_data <= err_char(send_idx);
               send_start <= 1'b1;
               send_idx <= send_idx + 8'd1;
            end else begin
               sending <= 1'b0;
            end
         end else if (send_idx < 8'd69) begin
            send_data <= report_char(send_idx);
            send_start <= 1'b1;
            send_idx <= send_idx + 8'd1;
         end else begin
            sending <= 1'b0;
         end
`endif
@

Finally the byte stream feeds a `uart_tx` (115200 baud at 12 MHz is 104
clocks per bit), whose line drives the `tx` pad through an `SB_IO` whose
output enable is `tx_oe` --- tri-stating the pad until the first report.

<<sport_rx: the UART and tx pad>>=
   uart_tx #(.CLKS_PER_BIT(104)) u_tx (
      .clk(clk12),
      .start(send_start),
      .data(send_data),
      .tx(uart_tx_line),
      .busy(uart_busy)
   );

   SB_IO #(
      .PIN_TYPE(6'b101001)
   ) tx_iob (
      .PACKAGE_PIN(tx),
      .OUTPUT_ENABLE(tx_oe),
      .D_OUT_0(uart_tx_line),
      .D_IN_0()
   );
@

=== The bidirectional top

`sport_bidir` is the one top the missions synthesize. It instantiates
`TX_TO_DSP_N` transmit lanes (F2D) and an `RX_FROM_DSP_N`-lane receiver
(D2F), so a build is a pure transmitter (`RX_FROM_DSP_N` used only as
unverified clock scaffolding), a pure receiver, or a bidirectional mix,
purely by parameter. `FROM_DSP_EN` gates the receiver: when zero the
D2F lanes exist only to carry the DSP's forwarded clock and the
receiver is held in reset, printing and claiming nothing. `SYNC_TX`
chooses the transmitter strategy --- the no-PLL source-synchronous
streamer when set (with `NOPLL`), the free-running PLL serializer when
clear --- and `SHARE_PAIRS` selects 4-lane pad sharing.

The eight shipped configurations and the parameter settings that select
each (every shipping build uses `SYNC_TX`=`NOPLL`=1, the no-PLL
transmitter):

[cols="<,^,^,^,^,^,^,^",options="header"]
|===
| config | TX_TO_DSP_N | RX_FROM_DSP_N | FROM_DSP_EN | SYNC_TX | NOPLL | SHARE_PAIRS | SHARE_COPIES
| D2F 1-lane    | 1 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 0
| D2F 2-lane    | 1 | 2 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 0
| D2F 4-lane    | 1 | 4 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 0
| F2D 1-lane    | 1 | 1 | 0 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 0
| F2D 2-lane    | 2 | 2 | 0 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 0
| F2D 4-lane    | 4 | 2 | 0 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 1
| bidir 1x1     | 1 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 0
| bidir 2x2     | 2 | 2 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 0
|===

(`SHARE_COPIES` is a `define`, not a parameter --- it adds the two SRU
clock-copy input pads the 4-lane F2D build launches its forwarders
from.)

<<sport_bidir.v>>=
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
// Copyright (c) 2026 Jakob Kastelic
module sport_bidir #(
      parameter TX_TO_DSP_N = 1,
      parameter RX_FROM_DSP_N = 1,
      parameter MIN_DONE_WORDS = 32'h00b00000,
      parameter REPORT_LANE0 = 0,
      parameter REPORT_IDX = 0,
      parameter TX_QUIET = 0,
      parameter SYNC_TX = 0,
      parameter NOPLL = 0,
      parameter NOPLL_DATA_NEG = 1,
      parameter NOPLL_CLK_POS = 1,
      parameter SHARE_PAIRS = 0,
      parameter FROM_DSP_EN = 1
   )(
      input clk12,
      input run,
      output [TX_TO_DSP_N-1:0] fpga_ad0_out,
      output [(SHARE_PAIRS ? TX_TO_DSP_N/2 : TX_TO_DSP_N)-1:0] fpga_aclk_out,
      output [(SHARE_PAIRS ? TX_TO_DSP_N/2 : TX_TO_DSP_N)-1:0] fpga_afs_out,
      output led,
      input  [RX_FROM_DSP_N-1:0] dsp_ad0_in,
      input  [RX_FROM_DSP_N-1:0] dsp_aclk_in,
`ifdef SHARE_COPIES
      input  dsp_aclk2_in,
      input  dsp_aclk3_in,
`endif
      input  [RX_FROM_DSP_N-1:0] dsp_afs_in,
      inout tx
   );
   wire [TX_TO_DSP_N-1:0] tx_ad0_w;
   wire [TX_TO_DSP_N-1:0] tx_aclk_w;
   wire [TX_TO_DSP_N-1:0] tx_afs_w;

   // Only consumed on the free-running (SYNC_TX==0) transmitter path; every
   // shipping config sets SYNC_TX=1, so this is inert in practice.
   localparam START_DELAY_CYCLES = 24000000;

   <<sport_bidir: generate the transmit lanes>>
   <<sport_bidir: map lanes to output pads>>
   <<sport_bidir: instantiate the from_dsp receiver>>
endmodule
@

The transmit side is one `generate`. With `SYNC_TX` and `NOPLL` set,
each lane gets its own `sport_tx_sync_nopll` clocked off a forwarded
DSP clock. Which forwarded clock matters for the 4-lane builds: each
forwarder must launch from the same physical clock net that feeds the
`from_dsp` capture registers for its lane, because mixing clock loads
shifts the per-netlist launch/capture phase. The plain case takes each
lane's primary `dsp_aclk_in[tx_i]`; under `SHARE_COPIES` both 4-lane
pairs launch from the single clean SRU copy `dsp_aclk3_in` (T8). The
other copy `dsp_aclk2_in` (P10) is the SPT4A "shield" tap on DAI1_04
(adjacent to RUN/R10) and is NOT a usable forwarding clock: a forwarder
launched off P10 mis-samples ~100% on both of its lanes at every bit
rate (45-59 MHz) -- the FFFF-DDDD lanes-0,2 failure -- while T8 is clean
for both DAI groups (bench-confirmed by swapping the copies, which flips
the failure to the T8->P10 pair). P10 stays DSP-driven as a shield, but
the FPGA no longer reads it. With `SYNC_TX` clear, a single
free-running `sport_tx_prbs_ser` drives all lanes.

<<sport_bidir: generate the transmit lanes>>=
generate
   if (SYNC_TX != 0) begin : sync_tx
      if (NOPLL != 0) begin : nopll
         genvar tx_i;
         for (tx_i = 0; tx_i < TX_TO_DSP_N; tx_i = tx_i + 1) begin : lanes
            sport_tx_sync_nopll #(
               .DATA_NEG(NOPLL_DATA_NEG),
               .CLK_POS(NOPLL_CLK_POS)
            ) to_dsp (
               .fwd_aclk(
`ifdef SHARE_COPIES
                         SHARE_PAIRS != 0
                         ? dsp_aclk3_in :
`endif
                         dsp_aclk_in[tx_i]),
               .run(run),
               .ad0_out(tx_ad0_w[tx_i]),
               .aclk_out(tx_aclk_w[tx_i]),
               .afs_out(tx_afs_w[tx_i])
            );
         end
      end
   end else begin : free_tx
      sport_tx_prbs_ser #(
         .N(TX_TO_DSP_N),
         .START_DELAY_CYCLES(START_DELAY_CYCLES),
         .TX_QUIET(TX_QUIET)
      ) to_dsp (
         .clk12(clk12),
         .ad0_out(tx_ad0_w),
         .aclk_out(tx_aclk_w),
         .afs_out(tx_afs_w)
      );
   end
endgenerate
@

Data goes out one pad per lane. The clock and frame-sync pads are
per-lane in the flat case, but under `SHARE_PAIRS` a lane pair shares
one clk/fs pad, driven by the even lane of the pair --- this is the
4-lane layout where two lanes ride a common forwarded clock/frame.

<<sport_bidir: map lanes to output pads>>=
assign fpga_ad0_out  = tx_ad0_w;
generate
   if (SHARE_PAIRS != 0) begin : pairpads
      genvar pp;
      for (pp = 0; pp < TX_TO_DSP_N/2; pp = pp + 1) begin : pairs
         assign fpga_aclk_out[pp] = tx_aclk_w[2*pp];
         assign fpga_afs_out[pp]  = tx_afs_w[2*pp];
      end
   end else begin : flatpads
      assign fpga_aclk_out = tx_aclk_w;
      assign fpga_afs_out  = tx_afs_w;
   end
endgenerate
@

The receiver is the previous section's `sport_rx`, wired to the D2F
input pads. It always runs in re-acquire mode (`RESYNC`). The run-seen-low
gate inside `sport_rx` is always active --- it self-arms once `run` has
genuinely been seen low --- and is harmless here because the on-chip
`run` is driven low at start rather than floating up from a boot bus.
`FROM_DSP_EN` masks `run` off when the D2F lanes are only scaffolding.

<<sport_bidir: instantiate the from_dsp receiver>>=
sport_rx #(.N(RX_FROM_DSP_N), .MIN_DONE_WORDS(MIN_DONE_WORDS),
           .REPORT_LANE0(REPORT_LANE0), .REPORT_IDX(REPORT_IDX),
           .RESYNC(1)) from_dsp (
   .led(led),
   .clk12(clk12),
   .aclk_in(dsp_aclk_in),
   .ad0_in(dsp_ad0_in),
   .afs_in(dsp_afs_in),
   .run(FROM_DSP_EN ? run : 1'b0),
   .tx(tx)
);
@

=== Test bench

The bench drives behavioural models of the DSP's SPORT transmit waveform
into the receiver and self-checks. It runs four sub-tests under
Verilator the same way every other chapter's bench does:

  1. *`RESYNC=0` lane.* The non-re-syncing per-lane checker locks on a
     single arming pulse and reports zero word errors --- the original
     coverage.

  2. *`RESYNC=1` lane.* The re-acquiring checker (the configuration the
     bidirectional builds actually ship) locks on a held frame-sync and
     reports zero errors.

  3. *Negative control.* A `RESYNC=1` lane fed a stream with one flipped
     bit must report `errors > 0`, proving the checker can *detect*
     errors, not merely avoid false positives.

  4. *Two-lane wrapper.* A full `sport_rx` with `N=2` is driven on both
     lanes; the bench decodes its UART output and asserts the report
     line ends in `PASS`, exercising the loop-free lane aggregation and
     the character-by-character report formatter.

Two timing facts shape the per-bit stimulus. First, each bit is launched
on the rising edge of `aclk` so it is stable across the receiver's
falling-edge sample --- there is no setup/hold race, and exactly one bit
advances per `aclk` period. Second, the frame-sync convention differs
between the two checker modes, which sets how each test frames its words
(below).

The `RESYNC=0` checker does not frame-lock precisely to `afs`: it arms on
the first frame-sync, waits a fixed three-edge internal latency, then
consumes one whole word to seed its PRBS engine before it begins
checking, and never re-syncs after that. So that test sends one arming
`afs` pulse, then `ARM_FILL` idle bits, where `ARM_FILL = 35 = 32 + 3`
--- one full word plus the three-bit arm-pipeline phase --- so the
receiver's first *checked* word lands exactly on the seed word of the
PRBS-31 stream. The `RESYNC=1` checker instead arms on any frame-sync
rise and aborts mid-word if frame-sync drops before bit 31, so its tests
hold `afs` high for the whole stream: the checker arms on the first rise
and then stays locked, dropping no startup word, so the PRBS stream runs
straight from the seed with no filler.

The bench is a logic check only. It runs with ideal, glitch-free timing
and a short `MIN_DONE`, so it proves the lockstep comparator, the
re-acquire state machine, the aggregation fold, and the report formatter
are correct --- but it says nothing about the analog eye, the
source-synchronous launch phase, or sustained behaviour over a real
transfer. Those are the bench deliverable: a 2 GiB-per-lane,
zero-error run in each direction on the hardware (see the Summary).

<<tb_sport.sv>>=
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
// Copyright (c) 2026 Jakob Kastelic
`timescale 1ns/1ps

module tb_sport;
   <<tb_sport: parameters and clocks>>
   <<tb_sport: instantiate the receivers under test>>
   <<tb_sport: a PRBS-31 reference generator>>
   <<tb_sport: drive the modeled SPORT waveform>>
   <<tb_sport: a UART receive decoder>>
   <<tb_sport: the stimulus and self-checks>>
endmodule
@

The lanes sample on `aclk`'s falling edge and the data we launch lives
in `ad0`/`afs`; `clk12` is the slow domain the receivers report into. The
`RESYNC=1` tests drive a second pair `ad0b`/`afsb`, and the two-lane
wrapper its own `ad0w`/`afsw`, so each sub-test owns its stimulus.

<<tb_sport: parameters and clocks>>=
localparam [30:0] PRBS31_SEED = 31'h7fffffff;
localparam WORDS    = 600;
localparam MIN_DONE = 32'd512;
localparam ARM_FILL = 35;          // 32 (one word) + 3 (arm-pipeline phase)
localparam UART_CPB = 104;         // clk12 cycles per UART bit (115200 @ 12 MHz)

reg aclk = 1'b0;
always #8 aclk <= ~aclk;
reg clk12 = 1'b0;
always #40 clk12 <= ~clk12;

reg ad0 = 1'b0;        // RESYNC=0 lane stimulus
reg afs = 1'b0;
reg ad0b = 1'b0;       // RESYNC=1 lane / negative-control stimulus
reg afsb = 1'b0;
reg [1:0] ad0w = 2'b00; // two-lane wrapper stimulus
reg [1:0] afsw = 2'b00;
reg run = 1'b0;
@

We instantiate the `RESYNC=0` lane, the `RESYNC=1` lane, and a full
two-lane `sport_rx` wrapper. The wrapper's input IO registers use an
`SB_IO` primitive port the simulation `SB_IO` model does not carry, so
the bench compiles the wrapper with `NO_IOREG` (set on the Verilator
command line) to bypass them; the checker logic under test is unchanged.

<<tb_sport: instantiate the receivers under test>>=
wire [31:0] words;
wire [31:0] errors;
wire        done;
sport_rx_chan #(.MIN_DONE_WORDS(MIN_DONE)) dut (
   .clk12(clk12), .run(run), .aclk_in(aclk), .ad0_in(ad0),
   .afs_in(afs), .words(words), .errors(errors), .done(done));

wire [31:0] words_r;
wire [31:0] errors_r;
wire        done_r;
sport_rx_chan #(.MIN_DONE_WORDS(MIN_DONE), .RESYNC(1)) dut_r (
   .clk12(clk12), .run(run), .aclk_in(aclk), .ad0_in(ad0b),
   .afs_in(afsb), .words(words_r), .errors(errors_r), .done(done_r));

wire [31:0] words_n;
wire [31:0] errors_n;
wire        done_n;
reg         ad0n = 1'b0;
reg         afsn = 1'b0;
sport_rx_chan #(.MIN_DONE_WORDS(MIN_DONE), .RESYNC(1)) dut_n (
   .clk12(clk12), .run(run), .aclk_in(aclk), .ad0_in(ad0n),
   .afs_in(afsn), .words(words_n), .errors(errors_n), .done(done_n));

wire tx_w;
wire led_w;
pullup(tx_w);
sport_rx #(.N(2), .MIN_DONE_WORDS(MIN_DONE), .RESYNC(1)) dut_w (
   .clk12(clk12), .aclk_in({aclk, aclk}), .ad0_in(ad0w), .afs_in(afsw),
   .run(run), .tx(tx_w), .led(led_w));

integer wi, bi;
reg [31:0] cur;
reg [30:0] prbs_state;
@

`prbs31_word` expands a 31-bit state into the next 32 MSB-first PRBS-31
bits; `prbs31_advance` returns the state 32 bits later. Both use the
same `x^31 + x^28 + 1` recurrence as the receiver.

<<tb_sport: a PRBS-31 reference generator>>=
function [31:0] prbs31_word(input [30:0] s_in);
   integer i; reg [30:0] s; reg [31:0] w; reg nb;
   begin
      s = s_in; w = 32'd0;
      for (i = 0; i < 32; i = i + 1) begin
         nb = s[30] ^ s[27];
         s = {s[29:0], nb};
         w = {w[30:0], nb};
      end
      prbs31_word = w;
   end
endfunction

function [30:0] prbs31_advance(input [30:0] s_in);
   integer i; reg [30:0] s; reg nb;
   begin
      s = s_in;
      for (i = 0; i < 32; i = i + 1) begin
         nb = s[30] ^ s[27];
         s = {s[29:0], nb};
      end
      prbs31_advance = s;
   end
endfunction
@

Each test has its own bit/word drivers. The `_a` tasks drive the
`RESYNC=0` lane (`afs` pulsed on bit 0). The `_b` tasks drive the
`RESYNC=1` lane with `afs` held high. The `_n` tasks drive a separate
`RESYNC=1` lane (`ad0n`/`afsn`) for the negative control, where
`send_word_flip_n` corrupts one bit. The `_w` task drives both wrapper
lanes identically with `afs` held high. Tasks use local loop variables
so concurrent drivers do not share counters.

<<tb_sport: drive the modeled SPORT waveform>>=
task send_bit_a(input val, input fsv);
   begin @(posedge aclk); ad0 = val; afs = fsv; end
endtask
task send_word_a(input [31:0] w);
   integer k;
   begin
      for (k = 0; k < 32; k = k + 1)
         send_bit_a(w[31-k], (k == 0) ? 1'b1 : 1'b0);
   end
endtask

task send_word_b(input [31:0] w);
   integer k;
   begin
      for (k = 0; k < 32; k = k + 1) begin
         @(posedge aclk); ad0b = w[31-k]; afsb = 1'b1;
      end
   end
endtask

task send_word_n(input [31:0] w);
   integer k;
   begin
      for (k = 0; k < 32; k = k + 1) begin
         @(posedge aclk); ad0n = w[31-k]; afsn = 1'b1;
      end
   end
endtask
task send_word_flip_n(input [31:0] w);
   integer k;
   begin
      for (k = 0; k < 32; k = k + 1) begin
         @(posedge aclk); ad0n = (k == 5) ? ~w[31-k] : w[31-k]; afsn = 1'b1;
      end
   end
endtask

task send_word_w(input [31:0] w);
   integer k;
   begin
      for (k = 0; k < 32; k = k + 1) begin
         @(posedge aclk); ad0w = {w[31-k], w[31-k]}; afsw = 2'b11;
      end
   end
endtask
@

The wrapper drives its report on the `tx` pad as 115200-baud UART. This
monitor decodes `tx` byte by byte (sampling the middle of each bit,
`UART_CPB` `clk12` ticks apart) and sets `wrapper_pass` once it sees the
four-byte `PASS` sequence that ends a passing report line.

<<tb_sport: a UART receive decoder>>=
reg        wrapper_pass = 1'b0;
reg [7:0]  uart_byte;
reg [1:0]  pass_match = 2'd0;
integer    ub;
initial begin
   forever begin
      @(negedge tx_w);                         // start bit
      repeat (UART_CPB + UART_CPB/2) @(posedge clk12);
      for (ub = 0; ub < 8; ub = ub + 1) begin  // 8 data bits, LSB first
         uart_byte[ub] = tx_w;
         repeat (UART_CPB) @(posedge clk12);
      end
      if (uart_byte == "P") pass_match = 2'd1;
      else if (uart_byte == "A" && pass_match == 2'd1) pass_match = 2'd2;
      else if (uart_byte == "S" && pass_match == 2'd2) pass_match = 2'd3;
      else if (uart_byte == "S" && pass_match == 2'd3) begin
         wrapper_pass = 1'b1; pass_match = 2'd0;
      end else pass_match = 2'd0;
   end
end
@

The stimulus runs the four sub-tests in sequence and ends with
`tb_sport: PASS` only if all four hold. A small `settle` helper drains
the report for a bounded number of `clk12` ticks; each test then checks
its own lane's `done` and word/error counts and `$fatal`s if the lane
never finished, so a hang fails loudly instead of passing silently.

<<tb_sport: the stimulus and self-checks>>=
<<tb_sport: settle helper>>
initial begin
   ad0 = 1'b0; afs = 1'b0; ad0b = 1'b0; afsb = 1'b0;
   ad0n = 1'b0; afsn = 1'b0; ad0w = 2'b00; afsw = 2'b00; run = 1'b0;
   repeat (4) @(posedge aclk);
   run = 1'b1;

   <<tb_sport: test 1 --- RESYNC=0 lane>>
   <<tb_sport: test 2 --- RESYNC=1 lane>>
   <<tb_sport: test 3 --- negative control>>
   <<tb_sport: test 4 --- two-lane wrapper>>

   $display("tb_sport: PASS");
   $finish;
end
@

`settle` simply advances `clk12` for a bounded number of ticks so the
lane's report can drain into the count registers; the per-test
`done`/error checks below turn a hang into a clear failure rather than a
silent zero-word pass.

<<tb_sport: settle helper>>=
task settle(input integer max_ticks);
   integer t;
   begin
      t = 0;
      while (t < max_ticks) begin
         @(posedge clk12);
         t = t + 1;
      end
   end
endtask
@

Test 1: the `RESYNC=0` lane. Arming pulse, `ARM_FILL` filler, then PRBS
from the seed; expect `done` with zero errors.

<<tb_sport: test 1 --- RESYNC=0 lane>>=
   send_bit_a(1'b0, 1'b1);
   for (bi = 0; bi < ARM_FILL; bi = bi + 1) send_bit_a(1'b0, 1'b0);
   prbs_state = PRBS31_SEED;
   for (wi = 0; wi < WORDS; wi = wi + 1) begin
      cur = prbs31_word(prbs_state);
      prbs_state = prbs31_advance(prbs_state);
      send_word_a(cur);
   end
   @(posedge aclk); ad0 = 1'b0; afs = 1'b0;
   settle(2000);
   $display("RESULT r0 words=%0d errors=%0d done=%0d", words, errors, done);
   if (!done || words < MIN_DONE)
      $fatal(1, "tb_sport t1: receiver did not finish (%0d/%0d words)", words, MIN_DONE);
   if (errors != 32'd0)
      $fatal(1, "tb_sport t1: %0d word errors", errors);
@

Test 2: the `RESYNC=1` lane. Frame-sync held high, PRBS from the seed
with no filler; expect `done` with zero errors.

<<tb_sport: test 2 --- RESYNC=1 lane>>=
   prbs_state = PRBS31_SEED;
   for (wi = 0; wi < WORDS; wi = wi + 1) begin
      cur = prbs31_word(prbs_state);
      prbs_state = prbs31_advance(prbs_state);
      send_word_b(cur);
   end
   @(posedge aclk); ad0b = 1'b0; afsb = 1'b0;
   settle(2000);
   $display("RESULT r1 words=%0d errors=%0d done=%0d", words_r, errors_r, done_r);
   if (!done_r || words_r < MIN_DONE)
      $fatal(1, "tb_sport t2: RESYNC lane did not finish (%0d/%0d words)", words_r, MIN_DONE);
   if (errors_r != 32'd0)
      $fatal(1, "tb_sport t2: %0d word errors", errors_r);
@

Test 3: the negative control drives a dedicated third `RESYNC=1` lane
(`dut_n`) with the same PRBS but one flipped bit mid-run. The lane must
still finish but report at least one error.

<<tb_sport: test 3 --- negative control>>=
   prbs_state = PRBS31_SEED;
   for (wi = 0; wi < WORDS; wi = wi + 1) begin
      cur = prbs31_word(prbs_state);
      prbs_state = prbs31_advance(prbs_state);
      if (wi == 100) send_word_flip_n(cur);
      else           send_word_n(cur);
   end
   @(posedge aclk); ad0n = 1'b0; afsn = 1'b0;
   settle(2000);
   $display("RESULT neg words=%0d errors=%0d done=%0d", words_n, errors_n, done_n);
   if (!done_n)
      $fatal(1, "tb_sport t3: negative control did not finish");
   if (errors_n == 32'd0)
      $fatal(1, "tb_sport t3: injected error went undetected");
@

Test 4: the two-lane wrapper. Both lanes carry the same valid PRBS with
frame-sync held high; after both lock and the aggregated report goes out,
the UART monitor must have seen `PASS`.

<<tb_sport: test 4 --- two-lane wrapper>>=
   prbs_state = PRBS31_SEED;
   for (wi = 0; wi < WORDS; wi = wi + 1) begin
      cur = prbs31_word(prbs_state);
      prbs_state = prbs31_advance(prbs_state);
      send_word_w(cur);
   end
   @(posedge aclk); ad0w = 2'b00; afsw = 2'b00;
   settle(300000);
   $display("RESULT wrapper pass_seen=%0d", wrapper_pass);
   if (!wrapper_pass)
      $fatal(1, "tb_sport t4: two-lane wrapper never reported PASS");
@

=== Pin maps

`sport_bidir` is one module, but the missions wire it to the board in a
few fixed ways, one constraint file per lane count and direction. Four
balls are common to all of them: the 12 MHz reference `clk12` on J3,
`run` on R10, the UART `tx` on B12 (which shares a net with the DSP
boot bus, the reason the receiver only ever drives it once it has
something to report), and `led` on B5. The lane balls follow one rule:
a D2F lane carries the DSP's SPORT A-half transmit out to a `from_dsp`
input (`dsp_*_in`), and an F2D lane carries a `to_dsp` output
(`fpga_*_out`) to the ball wired to a DSP SPORT B-half receive pin.

The 1x1 bidirectional map has one lane each way. The F2D lane drives
the DSP's SPORT5B receiver via DAI1 9/10/11 on T14/T15/T16; the D2F
lane is the DSP's SPORT4A transmit arriving on DAI1 5/7/8 at
N10/T11/T13.

<<sport_bidir_1x1_hx8k.pcf>>=
set_io clk12 J3
set_io fpga_ad0_out  T14
set_io fpga_aclk_out T15
set_io fpga_afs_out  T16
set_io dsp_ad0_in    N10
set_io dsp_aclk_in   T11
set_io dsp_afs_in    T13
set_io tx            B12
set_io run     R10
set_io led B5
@

The 2x2 map doubles that: lane 1 adds an F2D output to SPORT1B on DAI0
10/12/20 (P8/P9/T8) and a D2F input from SPORT0A on DAI0 5/7/8
(R5/T5/R9).

<<sport_bidir_2x2_hx8k.pcf>>=
set_io clk12 J3
set_io fpga_ad0_out[0]  T14
set_io fpga_aclk_out[0] T15
set_io fpga_afs_out[0]  T16
set_io fpga_ad0_out[1]  P8
set_io fpga_aclk_out[1] P9
set_io fpga_afs_out[1]  T8
set_io dsp_ad0_in[0]    N10
set_io dsp_aclk_in[0]   T11
set_io dsp_afs_in[0]    T13
set_io dsp_ad0_in[1]    R5
set_io dsp_aclk_in[1]   T5
set_io dsp_afs_in[1]    R9
set_io tx               B12
set_io run     R10
set_io led B5
@

The 4-lane F2D map is the `SHARE_PAIRS` layout: two lanes per DAI share
one clock/frame pad and ride separate data wires. Pair 0 (DAI1) clocks
on T15/T16 with data on T14 and R16; pair 1 (DAI0) clocks on P9/T10
with data on P8 and R4. The DSP's two forwarded transmit clocks arrive
on T11 and T5, and their SRU copies (the `SHARE_COPIES` inputs) on P10
and T8; the `from_dsp` data/fs balls are stubs here because the
receiver is scaffolding-only in this direction.

<<sport_bidir_4x_hx8k.pcf>>=
set_io clk12 J3
set_io fpga_aclk_out[0] T15
set_io fpga_afs_out[0]  T16
set_io fpga_ad0_out[0]  T14
set_io fpga_ad0_out[1]  R16
set_io fpga_aclk_out[1] P9
set_io fpga_afs_out[1]  T10
set_io fpga_ad0_out[2]  P8
set_io fpga_ad0_out[3]  R4
set_io dsp_aclk_in[0]  T11
set_io dsp_aclk_in[1]  T5
set_io dsp_ad0_in[0]   N10
set_io dsp_afs_in[0]   T13
set_io dsp_ad0_in[1]   R5
set_io dsp_afs_in[1]   R9
set_io tx            B12
set_io run     R10
set_io led B5
set_io dsp_aclk2_in P10
set_io dsp_aclk3_in T8
@

The 4-lane D2F map is the mirror: a full four-lane `from_dsp` receiver.
Its four input lanes land on the same balls the standalone four-lane
receiver used (N10/T11/T13, R5/T5/R9, T14/T15/T16, P8/P9/T10), so the
DSP's four transmit lanes (SPORT 4A/0A/5A/1A) reach the proven pins.
The lone dummy `to_dsp` output is left unconstrained --- the DSP does
not receive in this direction, so `--pcf-allow-unconstrained` places it
on a free ball and nothing reads it.

<<sport_bidir_4xrx_hx8k.pcf>>=
set_io clk12          J3
set_io dsp_ad0_in[0]  N10
set_io dsp_aclk_in[0] T11
set_io dsp_afs_in[0]  T13
set_io dsp_ad0_in[1]  R5
set_io dsp_aclk_in[1] T5
set_io dsp_afs_in[1]  R9
set_io dsp_ad0_in[2]  T14
set_io dsp_aclk_in[2] T15
set_io dsp_afs_in[2]  T16
set_io dsp_ad0_in[3]  P8
set_io dsp_aclk_in[3] P9
set_io dsp_afs_in[3]  T10
set_io run            R10
set_io tx             B12
set_io led            B5
@

=== Build fragment

Every Verilog module and pin map above is tangled from this one chapter.
The top Makefile's generic rule only fires when an output's basename
equals the chapter name, which is true for none of them, so this
fragment supplies explicit rules. Two static pattern rules tangle each
Verilog file and each pcf from `src/sport.nw`, and the phony
`sport-verilog` collects them. A SPORT mission refreshes all of them
from the chapter with a single `make -C fpga sport-verilog` before it
synthesizes, so the bitstreams are always built from the literate source
rather than from stale checked-in copies.

The test bench (`tb/tb_sport.sv`) does match the chapter name, so the
Makefile's generic `tb/tb_%.sv` rule already tangles it; the `sim`
target compiles it against the receiver, the `uart_tx` the two-lane
wrapper instantiates, and the shared `SB_IO` pad model. The bench's
two-lane sub-test elaborates the full `sport_rx` wrapper, whose input IO
registers use the `SB_IO` `.INPUT_CLK` port the simulation model omits,
so the compile passes `+define+NO_IOREG` to bypass those registers in
simulation only (synthesis never sees the define, so the bitstreams keep
their IO flops). The receiver is a large multi-configuration module, so
the Verilator pass waives a handful of lint classes that are noise here:
`PINMISSING` (the behavioural `SB_IO` model exposes optional DDR pins
that single-rate instantiations leave open), plus `UNUSEDSIGNAL`,
`DECLFILENAME`, `PINCONNECTEMPTY`, `WIDTH`, `CASEINCOMPLETE`,
`UNOPTFLAT`, and `MULTIDRIVEN` from `sport_rx.v` and the bench.

<<sport.mk>>=
SPORT_V := verilog/sport_tx_sync_nopll.v verilog/sport_tx_prbs_ser.v \
           verilog/sport_rx.v verilog/sport_bidir.v
SPORT_PCF := verilog/sport_bidir_1x1_hx8k.pcf verilog/sport_bidir_2x2_hx8k.pcf \
             verilog/sport_bidir_4x_hx8k.pcf verilog/sport_bidir_4xrx_hx8k.pcf

$(SPORT_V): verilog/%.v: src/sport.nw | verilog
	cd verilog && tangle $*.v < ../$<

$(SPORT_PCF): verilog/%.pcf: src/sport.nw | verilog
	cd verilog && tangle $*.pcf < ../$<

.PHONY: sport-verilog
sport-verilog: $(SPORT_V) $(SPORT_PCF)

build/sport/sim: tb/tb_sport.sv verilog/sport_rx.v verilog/uart_tx.v verilog/SB_IO.v | build/sport
	cd build/sport && verilator --Mdir obj_dir -Wall -Wno-PINMISSING \
		-Wno-UNUSEDSIGNAL -Wno-DECLFILENAME -Wno-PINCONNECTEMPTY \
		-Wno-WIDTH -Wno-CASEINCOMPLETE -Wno-UNOPTFLAT -Wno-MULTIDRIVEN \
		+define+NO_IOREG --binary \
		--top-module tb_sport ../../tb/tb_sport.sv ../../verilog/sport_rx.v \
		../../verilog/uart_tx.v ../../verilog/SB_IO.v
	cd build/sport && obj_dir/Vtb_sport
	@touch $@

sim: build/sport/sim
@

=== Summary

The whole link is three building blocks and one top that wires them up
by parameter:

  - `sport_tx_sync_nopll` --- the *no-PLL source-synchronous*
    transmitter. Generates an F2D PRBS-31 lane clocked entirely off the
    DSP's forwarded clock, so it stays in lockstep with the DSP's
    receiver with no clock of its own. Every shipping mission uses this.

  - `sport_tx_prbs_ser` --- the older *free-running* transmitter that
    makes its own bit clock with a PLL. No mission selects it any more;
    it survives as the alternate clocking strategy (`SYNC_TX` 0).

  - `sport_rx` --- the D2F *receiver/verifier*. Samples a DSP-driven
    PRBS-31 lane on the forwarded clock, reassembles 32-bit words,
    compares each against a locally stepped LFSR, and prints one
    PASS/FAIL line over UART once the link goes idle.

`sport_bidir` selects a direction and lane count purely through
parameters --- the same eight shipped configurations the missions
synthesize:

[cols="<,^,^,^,^,^,^,^",options="header"]
|===
| config | TX_TO_DSP_N | RX_FROM_DSP_N | FROM_DSP_EN | SYNC_TX | NOPLL | SHARE_PAIRS | SHARE_COPIES
| D2F 1-lane    | 1 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 0
| D2F 2-lane    | 1 | 2 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 0
| D2F 4-lane    | 1 | 4 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 0
| F2D 1-lane    | 1 | 1 | 0 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 0
| F2D 2-lane    | 2 | 2 | 0 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 0
| F2D 4-lane    | 4 | 2 | 0 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 1
| bidir 1x1     | 1 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 0
| bidir 2x2     | 2 | 2 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 0
|===

For an F2D build the receiver is present only as clock scaffolding
(`FROM_DSP_EN` 0 holds it in reset); for a D2F build the lone `to_dsp`
output is an unconstrained stub. `MIN_DONE_WORDS`, `REPORT_LANE0`, and
`REPORT_IDX` tune how much is transferred and whether the report folds
all lanes or just one. The `MIN_DONE_WORDS` default (`32'h00b00000`
$\approx$ 11.5 M words / 44 MiB) is only a placeholder; every mission
overrides it with the per-direction transfer size (e.g. 2 GiB/lane).

Verification is two-tier. The `tb_sport` bench drives a single
`sport_rx_chan` with a behavioural model of the DSP's transmit waveform
and confirms the lane locks and counts zero errors over `MIN_DONE`
words --- it covers one RX lane, the lockstep checker, and (with the
RESYNC test) the re-acquire path, in pure simulation. The real
deliverable is the bench proof: a 2 GiB-per-lane transfer in each
direction completing with `errors=0`, measured end-to-end by the
bench host's wall clock. The simulation guards the checker logic; the
hardware run is what actually qualifies a configuration.
